{:edb/db-before-t 2132 :edb/db-after-t 2133 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535445 50 #inst "2026-10-13T23:51:47.549Z" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186061945 1075 #edb/ref 17592186061946 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186061945 1075 #edb/ref 17592186061947 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186061945 1075 #edb/ref 17592186061948 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186061945 1075 #edb/ref 17592186061949 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186061945 1075 #edb/ref 17592186061950 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186061945 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504044 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186061945 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504101 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186061945 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504150 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186061945 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504156 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186061945 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504223 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186061945 1078 #edb/ref 17592186504156 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186064033 1071 "The function $g(x)$ is given by\n![](images/c6/c67122865cc8decbb3238cd70c1fe662bdbcf9f697bd8a8e594e4408b88a08c3.svg)\nIf $g(x)$ is continuous at $x=2,$ what is the value $b?$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186064033 1071 "The function $g(x)$ is given by\n${\\sqrt{bx + 3},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ne 2, \\\\ 5,\\end{vmatrix}x = 2$.\nIf $g(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$, what is the value $b$?" 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186064033 1074 "From the definition of $g(x)$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}g(x)={lim}_{x\\to 2}(\\sqrt{bx+3})=\\sqrt{2b+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}g(x) & =g(2) \\\\ \\sqrt{2b+3} & =5 \\\\ 2b+3 & =25 \\\\ 2b & =22 \\\\ b & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186064033 1074 "From the definition of $g(x)$,\n\n$$\nlim_(x → 2)g(x) = lim_(x → 2)(\\sqrt{bx + 3}) = \\sqrt{2b + 3}\n$$\n\nIn order for $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2$, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 2)g(x) &= g(2) \\\\\n\\sqrt{2b + 3} &= 5 \\\\\n2b + 3 &= 25 \\\\\n2b &= 22 \\\\\nb &= 11\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186064034 1071 "The function $h(x)$ is given by\n![](images/d1/d113c9de6e0c7946ed9952a2a245f4ba0ebb627b74c4661a2a067216fe5099d8.svg)\n\nGiven that $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=0,$ what is the value of $c?$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186064034 1071 "The function $h(x)$ is given by\n${\\log_{3} (x + c),\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ne 0, \\\\ 2,\\end{vmatrix}x = 0$.\n\nGiven that $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$, what is the value of $c$?" 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186064034 1074 "From the definition of $h(x)$, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 0)h(x) &= lim_(x → 0)\\log_{3} (x + c) \\\\\n&= \\log_{3} (0 + c) \\\\\n&= \\log_{3} (c)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $h(x)$ to be continuous at $x=0$, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 0)h(x) &= h(0) \\\\\n\\log_{3} (c) &= 2 \\\\\nc &= 3^{2} \\\\\nc &= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting $c=9$ removes the point discontinuity." 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186064034 1074 "From the definition of $h(x),$ we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}h(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}{log}_{3}⁡(x+c) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}⁡(0+c) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}⁡(c).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $h(x)$ to be continuous at $x=0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}h(x) & =h(0) \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡(c) & =2 \\\\ c & ={3}^{2} \\\\ c & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting $c=9$ removes the point discontinuity." 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186064118 1071 "The function $f(x)$ is defined for every real number $x$ and is given by\n![](images/90/90209edc9ee3cc9c6c17b71427dc4dda0a14558a9df7b2a71dbe017073b9086a.svg)\nIf $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1,$ what is the value of $k?$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186064118 1071 "The function $f(x)$ is defined for every real number $x$ and is given by\n${x^{2} + 2kx + 1,\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ne 1, \\\\ 4,\\end{vmatrix}x = 1$.\nIf $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$, what is the value of $k$?" 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186064118 1074 "From the definition of $f(x)$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 1)f(x) &= lim_(x → 1)(x^{2} + 2kx + 1) \\\\\n&= 1^{2} + 2k(1) + 1 \\\\\n&= 2 + 2k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=1$, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 1)f(x) &= f(1) \\\\\n2 + 2k &= 4 \\\\\n2k &= 2 \\\\\nk &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting $k=1$ removes the point discontinuity." 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186064118 1074 "From the definition of $f(x),$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,1}({x}^{2}+2kx+1) \\\\  & ={1}^{2}+2k(1)+1 \\\\  & =2+2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=1,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x) & =f(1) \\\\ 2+2k & =4 \\\\ 2k & =2 \\\\ k & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting $k=1$ removes the point discontinuity." 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186064143 1071 "Find the derivative of the function $y = x^{x^3}$." 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186064143 1071 "Find the derivative of the function $y = x^{x^3}.$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186064143 1074 "We have the function\n\n$$\ny = x^{x^3}\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ln y &= \\ln (x^{x^{3}}) \\\\\n\\ln y &= x^{3}\\ln x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\ln y) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{3}\\ln x) \\\\\n\\frac{1}{y} \\cdot y^{′} &= (x^{3})^{′} \\cdot (\\ln x) + (x^{3}) \\cdot (\\ln x)^{′} \\\\\n\\frac{1}{y} \\cdot y^{′} &= 3x^{2} \\cdot \\ln x + x^{3} \\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\\n\\frac{y^{′}}{y} &= 3x^{2}\\ln x + x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $y'{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= y(3x^{2}\\ln x + x^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute\n\n$$\ny=x^{x^3}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= x^{x^{3}}(3x^{2}\\ln x + x^{2}) \\\\\n&= x^{x^{3}}(x^{2}(3\\ln x + 1)) \\\\\n&= x^{x^{3} + 2}(3\\ln x + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186064143 1074 "We have the function \n\n$$\ny = x^{x^3}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{{x}^{3}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & ={x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =({x}^{3})^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+({x}^{3})\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =3{x}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $y'{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=x^{x^3}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{{x}^{3}}(3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2}) \\\\  & ={x}^{{x}^{3}}({x}^{2}(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)) \\\\  & ={x}^{{x}^{3}+2}(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186064144 1071 "Find the derivative of the function $y = (x^2-1)^x$." 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186064144 1071 "Find the derivative of the function $y = (x^2-1)^x.$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186064144 1074 "We have the function\n\n$$\ny = (x^2-1)^x\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ln y &= \\ln ((x^{2} - 1)^{x}) \\\\\n\\ln y &= x\\ln (x^{2} - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\ln y) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(x\\ln (x^{2} - 1)) \\\\\n\\frac{1}{y} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(x) \\cdot \\ln (x^{2} - 1) + x \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(\\ln (x^{2} - 1)) \\\\\n\\frac{1}{y} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} &= 1 \\cdot \\ln (x^{2} - 1) + x \\cdot (\\frac{1}{x^{2} - 1} \\cdot 2x) \\\\\n\\frac{1}{y} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\ln (x^{2} - 1) + \\frac{2x^{2}}{x^{2} - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= y(\\ln (x^{2} - 1) + \\frac{2x^{2}}{x^{2} - 1})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute\n\n$$\ny = (x^2-1)^x{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= (x^{2} - 1)^{x}(\\ln (x^{2} - 1) + \\frac{2x^{2}}{x^{2} - 1}) \\\\\n&= (x^{2} - 1)^{x}(((x^{2} - 1)\\ln (x^{2} - 1))/(x^{2} - 1) + \\frac{2x^{2}}{x^{2} - 1}) \\\\\n&= (x^{2} - 1)^{x - 1}((x^{2} - 1)\\ln (x^{2} - 1) + 2x^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186064144 1074 "We have the function \n\n$$\ny = (x^2-1)^x.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}-1)^{x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-1)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-1)+x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-1)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-1)+x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-1}\\cdot 2x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-1)+\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-1)+\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y = (x^2-1)^x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =({x}^{2}-1)^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-1)+\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-1}) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-1)^{x}(\\frac{({x}^{2}-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-1)}{{x}^{2}-1}+\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-1}) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-1)^{x-1}(({x}^{2}-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-1)+2{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186064145 1071 "Find the derivative of the function $y = (\\sin x)^{\\cos x}$." 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186064145 1071 "Find the derivative of the function $y = (\\sin x)^{\\cos x}.$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186064145 1074 "We have the function\n\n$$\ny = (\\sin x)^{\\cos x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ln y &= \\ln ((\\sin x)^{\\cos x}) \\\\\n\\ln y &= \\cos x\\ln (\\sin x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\ln y) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\cos x\\ln (\\sin x)) \\\\\n\\frac{1}{y} \\cdot y^{′} &= (\\cos x)^{′} \\cdot (\\ln (\\sin x)) + (\\cos x) \\cdot (\\ln (\\sin x))^{′} \\\\\n\\frac{1}{y} \\cdot y^{′} &= (-\\sin x) \\cdot \\ln (\\sin x) + \\cos x \\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\sin x} \\cdot \\cos x) \\\\\n\\frac{y^{′}}{y} &= \\cos x\\cot x - \\sin x\\ln (\\sin x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $y'{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= y(\\cos x\\cot x - \\sin x\\ln (\\sin x))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute\n\n$$\ny = (\\sin x)^{\\cos x}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= (\\sin x)^{\\cos x}(\\cos x\\cot x - \\sin x\\ln (\\sin x))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186064145 1074 "We have the function \n\n$$\ny = (\\sin x)^{\\cos x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x))+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x))}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $y'{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y = (\\sin x)^{\\cos x}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x))\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186064150 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504601 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186064150 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504606 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186064150 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504607 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186064150 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504621 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186064150 1038 #edb/r