6045485 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186064648 1071 "Consider the function $y = (2x)^{x}$. Its derivative is given by\n\n$y^{′} = (2x)^{x} \\cdot g(x)$.\n\nFind the function $g(x)$." 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186064648 1071 "Consider the function $y=(2x)^{x}.$ Its derivative is given by \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(2x)^{x}\\cdot g(x).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186064648 1074 "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=(2x)^{x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((2x)^{x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(x)^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x))+(x)\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x))}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2x}\\cdot 2) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=y=(2x)^{x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(2x)^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+1.$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186065391 1064 "Consider the function $f(x)$ below.\n\n$$\n{3x + a,\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ne 4, \\\\ 13,\\end{vmatrix}x = 4\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ seems to have a point discontinuity at $x=4$. However, by selecting a particular value for $a$, we can remove the point discontinuity and make the function continuous.\n\nWe can find this value of $a$ by forcing the limit of the function to be equal to the value of the function at $x=4$:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 4} f(x) = f(4)\n$$\n\nRemember that $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 4} f(x)$ represents the value that $f(x)$ approaches as $x$ gets closer and closer, but not equal, to $4$. So, when evaluating $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 4} f(x)$, we need to use the function definition for\n\n$$\nx \\neq 4\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 4)f(x) &= lim_(x → 4)(3x + a) \\\\\n&= 3(4) + a \\\\\n&= 12 + a\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $x=4$, the function is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=13\n$$\n\nSo\n\n$$\nf(4)=13\n$$\n\nand we can solve the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 4)f(x) &= f(4) \\\\\n12 + a &= 13 \\\\\na &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to make $f(x)$ continuous at $x=4$, we need to choose $a=1$." 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186065391 1064 "Consider the function $f(x)$ below.\n![](images/20/20b2fd99ae051c3447e4c3530fee053b248393147a8295d3a9451524d7534f52.svg)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ seems to have a point discontinuity at $x=4.$ However, by selecting a particular value for $a,$ we can remove the point discontinuity and make the function continuous.\n\nWe can find this value of $a$ by forcing the limit of the function to be equal to the value of the function at $x=4\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 4} f(x) = f(4)\n$$\n\nRemember that $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 4} f(x)$ represents the value that $f(x)$ approaches as $x$ gets closer and closer, but not equal, to $4.$ So, when evaluating $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 4} f(x),$ we need to use the function definition for $x \\neq 4.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 4}(3x+a) \\\\  & =3(4)+a \\\\  & =12+a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $x=4,$ the function is defined as $f(x)=13.$ So $f(4)=13,$ and we can solve the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x) & =f(4) \\\\ 12+a & =13 \\\\ a & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to make $f(x)$ continuous at $x=4,$ we need to choose $a=1.$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186065395 1064 "**Logarithmic differentiation** is a technique to compute derivatives that are difficult to find using the usual rules of differentiation, such as the product, quotient, and chain rules.\n\nLet's consider the following function.\n\n$$\ny = x^x\n$$\n\nWe aim to compute\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\n$$\n\nHowever, in this case, we note the following:\n\n- Both the base and the exponent are functions of $x$.\n- This means we cannot apply the power rule since the exponent is not a fixed number.\n- We also cannot apply the rules for differentiating exponentials since the base is not a fixed number.\n\nSo instead, we'll use logarithmic differentiation. We proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1.** We take the natural logarithm of both sides and apply the power rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= x^{x} \\\\\n\\ln y &= \\ln x^{x} \\\\\n\\ln y &= x\\ln x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** We differentiate both sides of the above equation with respect to $x$ using implicit differentiation and the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\ln y) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(x\\ln x) \\\\\n\\frac{1}{y} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(x) \\cdot \\ln x + x \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(\\ln x) \\\\\n\\frac{1}{y} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} &= 1 \\cdot \\ln x + x \\cdot (\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\\n\\frac{1}{y} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\ln x + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Now, we solve for\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = y\\left(\\ln x + 1 \\right)\n$$\n\n**Step 4.** Finally, we substitute back our original expression for $y=x^x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = x^x \\left(\\ln x + 1 \\right)\n$$\n\nLet's see another example." 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186065395 1064 "**Logarithmic differentiation** is a technique to compute derivatives that are difficult to find using the usual rules of differentiation, such as the product, quotient, and chain rules.\n\nLet's consider the following function.\n\n$$\ny = x^x\n$$\n\nWe aim to compute $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}.$ However, in this case, we note the following:\n\n- Both the base and the exponent are functions of $x.$\n\n- This means we cannot apply the power rule since the exponent is not a fixed number.\n\n- We also cannot apply the rules for differentiating exponentials since the base is not a fixed number.\n\nSo instead, we'll use logarithmic differentiation. We proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1.** We take the natural logarithm of both sides and apply the power rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={x}^{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** We differentiate both sides of the above equation with respect to $x$ using implicit differentiation and the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x+x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x+x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Now, we solve for $\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} =  y\\left( \\ln x + 1 \\right).\n$$\n\n**Step 4.** Finally, we substitute back our original expression for $y=x^x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = x^x \\left( \\ln x + 1 \\right)\n$$\n\nLet's see another example." 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186096630 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504624 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186096630 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504695 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186096631 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504632 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186096631 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504672 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186096632 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504619 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186096632 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504654 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186096632 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504658 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186096633 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504609 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186096633 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504685 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186096633 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504688 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186096633 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504700 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186096875 1050 960.0 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186097447 1061 1457 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186097447 1061 631552 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186097448 1061 1458 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186097448 1061 631554 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186097449 1061 1461 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186097449 1061 631553 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186097450 1061 1462 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186097450 1061 631557 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186097451 1061 1464 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186097451 1061 631560 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186097452 1061 1466 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186097452 1061 631563 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186097453 1061 1467 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186097453 1061 631564 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186097910 1064 "Suppose that we want to approximate the area $\\mathcal A$ bound by the curve $y= x^2+1$ and the lines $x=0,$ $x=1,$ and the $x$-axis. We can do this using a left Riemann sum with $n=4$ subintervals, as shown below:\n\n![](images/1b/1b25a722d5527db77cd7dd5fb581c0ffed9b9324913d390e2633211360b6a8ab.png)\n\nA better approximation can be found by taking more subintervals. For instance, taking $n=8$ subintervals gives:\n\n![](images/17/17f6c8e458b2e6fbd887728cbbcc7cb64a670598256f76cad69d9e5a9c3cf7b5.png)\n\nAs we continue to increase $n,$ our approximation gets better and better. In fact, we can get an *exact* value for the area by taking the limit of the Riemann sum as $n\\to\\infty.$\n\nLet's write down that limit. First, we need to express the left Riemann sum using sigma notation. Using $n$ subintervals between the endpoints $a=0$ and $b=1,$ we express\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & \\approx {\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f(0+k\\cdot Δx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f(k\\cdot Δx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}({(k\\cdot Δx)}^{2}+1)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}({k}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}(\\frac{{k}^{2}}{{n}^{2}}+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the above, we used the fact that $x_k = 0 + k\\Delta x = k\\Delta x$ and $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{1 - 0}{n} = \\dfrac 1 n,$ as we've seen before.\n\nThe exact area is found by computing the limit as $n\\to\\infty.$ So instead of using an approximation symbol $(\\approx),$ we can now use an equality symbol and write\n\n$$\nA = \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\\left(\\dfrac{k^2}{n^2} + 1\\right)\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{n}.\n$$\n\nThe formula above is a bit clunky, so instead of writing it using limits and sigma notation, we introduce a new notation called the **definite integral**, defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\\left(\\dfrac{k^2}{n^2} + 1\\right)\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{n}= \\int_0^1 (x^2 + 1)\\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our example, \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_0^1 (x^2+1)\\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\ncan be interpreted as the exact area under the graph of $y = f(x) = x^2+1$ between $x=0,$ $x=1,$ and the $x$-axis." 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186097910 1064 "Suppose that we want to approximate the area $\\mathcal A$ bound by the curve $y= x^2+1$ and the lines $x=0,$ $x=1,$ and the $x$-axis. We can do this using a left Riemann sum with $n=4$ subintervals, as shown below:\n\n![](images/1b/1b25a722d5527db77cd7dd5fb581c0ffed9b9324913d390e2633211360b6a8ab.png)\n\nA better approximation can be found by taking more subintervals. For instance, taking $n=8$ subintervals gives:\n\n![](images/17/17f6c8e458b2e6fbd887728cbbcc7cb64a670598256f76cad69d9e5a9c3cf7b5.png)\n\nAs we continue to increase $n,$ our approximation gets better and better. In fact, we can get an *exact* value for the area by taking the limit of the Riemann sum as $n\\to\\infty.$\n\nLet's write down that limit. First, we need to express the left Riemann sum using sigma notation. Using $n$ subintervals between the endpoints $a=0$ and $b=1,$ we express\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f(0+k\\cdot Δx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f(k\\cdot Δx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}({(k\\cdot Δx)}^{2}+1)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}({k}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}(\\frac{{k}^{2}}{{n}^{2}}+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the above, we used the fact that $x_k = 0 + k\\Delta x = k\\Delta x$ and $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{1 - 0}{n} = \\dfrac 1 n,$ as we've seen before.\n\nThe exact area is found by computing the limit as $n\\to\\infty.$ So instead of using an approximation symbol $(\\approx),$ we can now use an equality symbol and write\n\n$$\nA = \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\\left(\\dfrac{k^2}{n^2} + 1\\right)\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{n}.\n$$\n\nThe formula above is a bit clunky, so instead of writing it using limits and sigma notation, we introduce a new notation called the **definite integral**, defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\\left(\\dfrac{k^2}{n^2} + 1\\right)\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{n}= \\int_0^1 (x^2 + 1)\\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our example, \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_0^1 (x^2+1)\\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\ncan be interpreted as the exact area under the graph of $y = f(x) = x^2+1$ between $x=0,$ $x=1,$ and the $x$-axis." 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186097911 1064 "In general, for a function $f(x)$ defined on $[a,b],$ the **definite integral** of $f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be defined using the limit of a left  Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f(x)\\textrm{d}x = \\lim_{n\\rightarrow\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1} f(x_k)\\Delta x,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{b-a}{n}$ and $x_k = a + k\\Delta x.$\n\nThe limit $x=a$ is referred to as the **lower limit**, whereas $x=b$ is called the **upper limit**." 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186097911 1064 "In general, for a function $f(x)$ defined on $[a,b],$ the **definite integral** of $f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be defined using the limit of a left  Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f(x)\\textrm{d}x = \\lim_{n\\rightarrow\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1} f(x_k)\\Delta x,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{b-a}{n}$ and $x_k = a + k\\Delta x.$\n\nThe limit $x=a$ is referred to as the **lower limit**, whereas $x=b$ is called the **upper limit**." 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186119795 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504690 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186119795 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504696 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186119796 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504625 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186124136 1078 #edb/ref 17592186124139 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186124451 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504693 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186124459 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504655 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186124608 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504698 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186124610 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504665 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186125256 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186125256 1071 "Let $f(x, y)$ be a differentiable function such that\n$f(1, 1) = 1, f_{x}(x, y) = 3x^{2}y^{4}, f_{y}(x, y) = 4x^{3}y^{3}$.\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1, 1)$, estimate the value of $f(1.02, 0.97)$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186125256 1071 "Let $f(x,y)$ be a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(1,1)=1,\\,{f}_{x}(x,y)=3{x}^{2}{y}^{4},\\,{f}_{y}(x,y)=4{x}^{3}{y}^{3}.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,1),$ estimate the value of $f(1.02,0.97).$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186125256 1074 "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,1)$ is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(1,1)+∇f(1,1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-1⟩ \\\\  & ≈f(1,1)+⟨{f}_{x}(1,1),{f}_{y}(1,1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-1⟩ \\\\  & ≈1+⟨{f}_{x}(1,1),{f}_{y}(1,1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find ${f}_{x}(1,1)$ and ${f}_{y}(1,1)$ using the given partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,1) & =3(1)^{2}(1)^{4}=3 \\\\ {f}_{y}(1,1) & =4(1)^{3}(1)^{3}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈1+⟨3,4⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-1⟩\n$$\n\nFinally, we can estimate $f(1.02,0.97)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=1.02$ and $y=0.97,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.02,0.97) & ≈1+⟨3,4⟩\\cdot ⟨1.02-1,0.97-1⟩ \\\\  & =1+⟨3,4⟩\\cdot ⟨0.02,-0.03⟩ \\\\  & =1+0.06-0.12 \\\\  & =0.94\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186127711 1075 #edb/ref 17592186127712 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186127711 1075 #edb/ref 17592186127713 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186127711 1075 #edb/ref 17592186127714 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186127711 1075 #edb/ref 17592186127715 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186127711 1075 #edb/ref 17592186127716 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186127711 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504070 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186127711 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504187 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186127711 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504318 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186127711 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504332 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186127711 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504469 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186127711 1078 #edb/ref 17592186504070 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186128623 1075 #edb/ref 17592186128624 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186128623 1075 #edb/ref 17592186128625 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186128623 1075 #edb/ref 17592186128626 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186128623 1075 #edb/ref 17592186128627 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186128623 1075 #edb/ref 17592186128628 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186128623 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504067 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186128623 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504203 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186128623 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504210 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186128623 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504212 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186128623 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504252 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186128623 1078 #edb/ref 17592186504212 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186128903 1074 "The function has discontinuities due to vertical asymptotes at $x=\\pm 1$. Note that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{+}} f(x) = -\\infty\n$$\n\nwhich is enough to conclude that there is a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=-1$. Similarly\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 1^{-}} f(x) = \\infty\n$$\n\nwhich is enough to conclude that there is a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=1$.\n\nThere is also a discontinuity at $x=-2$, but this is a point discontinuity, not a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote." 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186128903 1074 "The function has discontinuities due to vertical asymptotes at $x=\\pm 1.$ Note that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{+}} f(x) = -\\infty,\n$$\n\nwhich is enough to conclude that there is a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=-1.$ Similarly\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 1^{-}} f(x) = \\infty,\n$$\n\nwhich is enough to conclude that there is a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=1.$\n\nThere is also a discontinuity at $x=-2,$ but this is a point discontinuity, not a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote." 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186128904 1074 "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x)$.\n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes, let's start by factoring the expression in the denominator. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\frac{1}{x^{2} - 5x + 6} \\\\\n&= (1)/((x - 3)(x - 2))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we can find the vertical asymptotes by setting the denominator to zero:\n\n$$\n(x-3)(x-2) = 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=2,3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has infinite discontinuities at $x=2$ and $x=3$." 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186128904 1074 "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ \n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes, let's start by factoring the expression in the denominator. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-5x+6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(x-3)(x-2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we can find the vertical asymptotes by setting the denominator to zero:\n\n$$\n(x-3)(x-2) = 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=2,3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has infinite discontinuities at $x=2$ and $x=3.$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186128905 1074 "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x)$.\n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes, let's start by factoring the expressions in the numerator and denominator. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\frac{x^{2} + x - 6}{x^{2} - 4} \\\\\n&= ((x - 2)(x + 3))/((x - 2)(x + 2)) \\\\\n&= ((x - 2)(x + 3))/((x - 2)(x + 2)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{x + 3}{x + 2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\nx+2 = 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=-2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has an infinite discontinuity at $x=-2$." 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186128905 1074 "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ \n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes, let's start by factoring the expressions in the numerator and denominator. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+x-6}{{x}^{2}-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-2)(x+3)}{(x-2)(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(x-2)}(x+3)}{\\cancel{(x-2)}(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+3}{x+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\nx+2 = 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has an infinite discontinuity at $x=-2.$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186128906 1071 "Find all of the points where $f(x)$ below has infinite discontinuities.\n\n![](images/2b/2b1f430a78861fce15c689843d0e4af5376e0f600b650b0a614257d86365cd29.svg)" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186128906 1071 "Find all of the points where $f(x)$ below has infinite discontinuities.\n\n${\\frac{1}{x - 5},\\begin{vmatrix}x < 5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{x - 4},\\end{vmatrix}x \\ge 5$." 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186128906 1074 "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x)$. The only possible way that $y=f(x)$ can have vertical asymptotes is if either\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{x-5}\n$$\n\nor\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{x-4}\n$$\n\ngrow without bound within their respective subdomains.\n\n- We first compute the asymptotes of $\\dfrac{1}{x-5}$. Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n$x-5=0\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=5$.\nAlthough this branch of the function is defined for $x < 5$, we do have that\n$\\lim_{x\\to 5^{-}}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{x-5}\\right) = -\\infty$,\nand therefore we conclude that $x=5$ is indeed an infinite discontinuity.\n- Next, we compute the asymptotes of $\\dfrac{1}{x-4}$. Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n$x-4=0\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=4$.\nHowever, this branch of the function is defined for $x \\geq 5$, which does not contain the neighborhood of $x=4$. So $x=4$ cannot be a vertical asymptote, and therefore $x=4$ cannot be an infinite discontinuity.\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ only has a single infinite discontinuity, and it is at $x=5$.\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $y=f(x)$, shown below.\n\n![](images/c1/c1e68cf3a87e981a07fdf6c8a2816ed25b26670b8afa274d95f05e7ea90ee340.png)" 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186128906 1074 "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ The only possible way that $y=f(x)$ can have vertical asymptotes is if either $\\dfrac{1}{x-5}$ or $\\dfrac{1}{x-4}$ grow without bound within their respective subdomains.\n\n- We first compute the asymptotes of $\\dfrac{1}{x-5}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\nx-5=0\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=5.\n$$\n\nAlthough this branch of the function is defined for $x < 5$, we do have that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 5^{-}}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{x-5}\\right) = -\\infty,\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=5$ is indeed an infinite discontinuity.\n\n- Next, we compute the asymptotes of $\\dfrac{1}{x-4}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\nx-4=0\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=4.\n$$\n\nHowever, this branch of the function is defined for $x \\geq 5,$ which does not contain the neighborhood of $x=4.$ So $x=4$ cannot be a vertical asymptote, and therefore $x=4$ cannot be an infinite discontinuity.\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ only has a single infinite discontinuity, and it is at $x=5.$\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $y=f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/c1/c1e68cf3a87e981a07fdf6c8a2816ed25b26670b8afa274d95f05e7ea90ee340.png)" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186128997 1074 "The tangent plane to a surface is horizontal if and only if the normal vector to the tangent plane is vertical. So, let's first find the general expression of a normal vector to the surface.\n\nRemember that the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz - z_0 = (x-x_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0) + (y-y_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\left\\langle \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0), \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0), -1 \\right\\rangle$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+xy) \\\\  & =y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+xy) \\\\  & =2y+x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n\\left\\langle y, \\: 2y+x, \\: -1 \\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nWe want to find where this normal vector is vertical. This means that the $x$- and $y$-components of the vector must be $0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/df/df6542baabd72b24ce115cb79c912c7b19fd3bd1b663467956445553b60a68c1.png) \n\nFrom the first equation, we get $y=0.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n2(0) +x = 0 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x = 0.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=0$ and $y=0$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz = 0^2+(0)(0)= 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point where the tangent plane is horizontal is $(0,0,0).$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186128997 1074 "The tangent plane to a surface is horizontal if and only if the normal vector to the tangent plane is vertical. So, let's first find the general expression of a normal vector to the surface.\n\nRemember that the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz - z_0 = (x-x_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0) + (y-y_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\left\\langle \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0), \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0), -1 \\right\\rangle$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+xy) \\\\  & =y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+xy) \\\\  & =2y+x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n\\left\\langle y, \\: 2y+x, \\: -1 \\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nWe want to find where this normal vector is vertical. This means that the $x$- and $y$-components of the vector must be $0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/ec/ec631b90dd9a037263e163bc32ebd982de5f60ee813622ae7b4f1a6b153fe856.svg) \n\nFrom the first equation, we get $y=0.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n2(0) +x = 0 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x = 0.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=0$ and $y=0$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz = 0^2+(0)(0)= 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point where the tangent plane is horizontal is $(0,0,0).$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186129089 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504622 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186129089 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504671 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186129089 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504678 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186129089 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504705 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186129090 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504626 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186129090 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504634 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186129090 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504679 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186129090 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504694 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186129090 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504701 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186129091 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504659 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186129091 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504664 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186129091 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504666 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186129091 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504683 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186129091 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504707 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186129092 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504614 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186129092 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504631 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186129092 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504667 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186129092 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504674 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186129092 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504708 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186129177 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504673 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186129178 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504627 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186129178 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504653 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186129178 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504691 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186129180 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504633 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186129328 1050 1260.0 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186129349 1050 540.0 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186129705 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186129705 1071 "![](images/74/744bb33e33eb84d02f53403b9f6ccd6077f6f9a09b5781ffdc31f61c6b5b8343.png)\n\nFor the function $y = f(x)$ above, where does the function have a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote?" 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186129705 1071 "![](images/74/744bb33e33eb84d02f53403b9f6ccd6077f6f9a09b5781ffdc31f61c6b5b8343.png)\n\nFor the function $y=f(x)$ above, where does the function have a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote?" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186129705 1074 "The function has a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=-1.$ Note that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}f(x)=\\infty ,\n$$\n\nwhich is enough to conclude that there is a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=-1.$\n\nThere are also discontinuities at $x=-2$ and $x=0,$ but they are not discontinuities due to vertical asymptotes." 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186129857 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186129857 1071 "Find the equation of the tangent hyperplane to the hypersurface $w = x^{2} - y^{2} + xyz$ at the point $(1, 1, 1, 1)$." 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186129857 1071 "Find the equation of the tangent hyperplane to the hypersurface $w={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+xyz$ at the point $(1,1,1,1).$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186129857 1074 "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the hypersurface $w=f(x,y,z)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0},{w}_{0})$ on the hypersurface can be written as\n\n$$\nw-{w}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(z-{z}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂z}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+xyz)=2x+yz \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+xyz)=-2y+xz \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+xyz)=xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y,z)=(1,1,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,1,1) & =2(1)+(1)(1)=3 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,1,1) & =-2(1)+(1)(1)=-1 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z}(1,1,1) & =(1)(1)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent hyperplane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w-1 & =(x-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,1,1)+(y-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,1,1)+(z-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂z}(1,1,1) \\\\ w-1 & =3(x-1)-(y-1)+(z-1) \\\\ w-1 & =3x-3-y+1+z-1 \\\\ -3x+y-z+w+2 & =0 \\\\ 3x-y+z-w-2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186130330 1064 "Let's take a look at the function\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac 1 x\n$$\n\nbelow.\n\n![](images/cc/cc9593860b0c134d0d99635b37eeac4f16a712a7af9e1c6474fcf1f885e8451e.png)\n\nThe function has a vertical asymptote at $x=0$. There is also a discontinuity in the function at $x=0$, since we cannot draw the points on the curve without taking our pen off the paper to cross the asymptote.\n\nSo, we say that\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac 1 x\n$$\n\nhas a **discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote** at $x=0$. Discontinuities due to vertical asymptotes are also called **infinite discontinuities**.\n\nNotice that here, both one-sided limits are infinite:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0^-} \\left(\\dfrac 1 x\\right) =-\\infty\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\\lim_{x\\to 0^+} \\left(\\dfrac 1 x \\right)=\\infty\n$$\n\nIn general, a function $y=f(x)$ has a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=c$ if either one-sided limit is infinite:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to c^-} f(x) =\\pm \\infty\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad\\lim_{x\\to c^+} f(x) =\\pm \\infty\n$$\n\nNote that we only need a single limit to become infinite to conclude that there is an infinite discontinuity at $x=c$." 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186130330 1064 "Let's take a look at the function $y=\\dfrac 1 x$ below.\n\n![](images/cc/cc9593860b0c134d0d99635b37eeac4f16a712a7af9e1c6474fcf1f885e8451e.png)\n\nThe function has a vertical asymptote at $x=0.$ There is also a discontinuity in the function at $x=0,$ since we cannot draw the points on the curve without taking our pen off the paper to cross the asymptote.\n\nSo, we say that $y = \\dfrac 1 x$ has a **discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote** at $x=0.$ Discontinuities due to vertical asymptotes are also called **infinite discontinuities**.\n\nNotice that here, both one-sided limits are infinite:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0^-} \\left(\\dfrac 1 x\\right) =-\\infty\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\\lim_{x\\to 0^+} \\left(\\dfrac 1 x \\right)=\\infty.\n$$\n\nIn general, a function $y=f(x)$ has a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=c$ if either one-sided limit is infinite:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to c^-} f(x) =\\pm \\infty\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad\\lim_{x\\to c^+} f(x) =\\pm \\infty.\n$$\n\nNote that we only need a single limit to become infinite to conclude that there is an infinite discontinuity at $x=c.$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186130382 1064 "Earlier, we claimed that function $f(x)$ is differentiable at $x_0$ *if the function admits a unique tangent line* at $x_0.$ Let's now justify this claim.\n\nRecall that a function $f(x)$ is differentiable at $x=x_0$ with derivative $f'(x_0)$ if and only if the following limit exists:\n\n$$\nf'(x_0) = \\lim_{x\\to x_0}\\dfrac {f(x) - f(x_0)} {x-x_0}\n$$\n\nWe can rewrite this limit as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {x}_{0}}(\\frac{f(x)-f({x}_{0})}{x-{x}_{0}})-{f}^{'}({x}_{0}) & =0 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {x}_{0}}(\\frac{f(x)-f({x}_{0})}{x-{x}_{0}}-{f}^{'}({x}_{0})) & =0 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {x}_{0}}\\frac{f(x)-f({x}_{0})-(x-{x}_{0}){f}^{'}({x}_{0})}{x-{x}_{0}} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for the above limit to hold true, the numerator must approach zero. So, near $x_0,$ we must have\n\n$$\nf(x) - f(x_0) - (x-x_0)f'(x_0) \\approx 0,\n$$\n\nwhich can be rewritten as\n\n$$\nf(x) - f(x_0) \\approx (x-x_0)f'(x_0).\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the following equation of the tangent line at $x_0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\ny -y_0 = (x-x_0)\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d x}(x_0)\n$$\n\nFrom here, we can say that a function $f(x)$ is differentiable at $x_0$ *if the function admits a unique tangent line* at $x_0.$\n\nWe'll now use a similar idea to define the differentiability of a multivariable function at a point $P$ in terms of tangent planes." 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186130382 1064 "Earlier, we claimed that function $f(x)$ is differentiable at $x_0$ *if the function admits a unique tangent line* at $x_0.$ Let's now justify this claim.\n\nRecall that a function $f(x)$ is differentiable at $x=x_0$ with derivative $f'(x_0)$ if and only if the following limit exists:\n\n$$\nf'(x_0) = \\lim_{x\\to x_0}\\dfrac {f(x) - f(x_0)} {x-x_0}\n$$\n\nWe can rewrite this limit as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {x}_{0}}(\\frac{f(x)-f({x}_{0})}{x-{x}_{0}})-{f}^{′}({x}_{0}) & =0 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {x}_{0}}(\\frac{f(x)-f({x}_{0})}{x-{x}_{0}}-{f}^{′}({x}_{0})) & =0 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {x}_{0}}\\frac{f(x)-f({x}_{0})-(x-{x}_{0}){f}^{′}({x}_{0})}{x-{x}_{0}} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for the above limit to hold true, the numerator must approach zero. So, near $x_0,$ we must have\n\n$$\nf(x) - f(x_0) - (x-x_0)f'(x_0) \\approx 0,\n$$\n\nwhich can be rewritten as\n\n$$\nf(x) - f(x_0) \\approx (x-x_0)f'(x_0).\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the following equation of the tangent line at $x_0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\ny -y_0 = (x-x_0)\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d x}(x_0)\n$$\n\nFrom here, we can say that a function $f(x)$ is differentiable at $x_0$ *if the function admits a unique tangent line* at $x_0.$\n\nWe'll now use a similar idea to define the differentiability of a multivariable function at a point $P$ in terms of tangent planes." 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186130386 1064 "Similar to tangent lines for single variable functions, we can think of the **tangent plane** to a surface at a point as the plane that \"touches\" the surface at that point.\n\nThe equation of the tangent plane to a surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $(x_0,y_0,z_0),$ where $z_0=f(x_0,y_0),$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nz - z_0 = (x-x_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0) + (y-y_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0).\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the surface \n\n$$\nz = 4-x^2-y^2.\n$$\n\nLet's find the equation of the tangent plane to the surface at the point $(0,\\sqrt{2},2),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/03/03df90a94615238e2126338f64d1f51fcefc379068a8cdc366b33b998cd69f45.png)\n\n \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y) = (0,\\sqrt{2})\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(0,\\sqrt{2}) & =-2(0)=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(0,\\sqrt{2}) & =-2(\\sqrt{2})=-2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-2 & =(x-0)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(0,\\sqrt{2})+(y-\\sqrt{2})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(0,\\sqrt{2}) \\\\ z-2 & =0(x-0)-2\\sqrt{2}(y-\\sqrt{2}) \\\\ z-2 & =-2\\sqrt{2}y+4 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2}y+z-6 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get that the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane is $2\\sqrt{2}y + z - 6 = 0.$\n\n![](images/d5/d51bdbe8820472334f7e1d942c027b6ad8164eaece58220f3f2e5cdb51544f05.png)" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186130386 1064 "Similar to tangent lines for single variable functions, we can think of the **tangent plane** to a surface at a point as the plane that \"touches\" the surface at that point.\n\nThe equation of the tangent plane to a surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $(x_0,y_0,z_0),$ where $z_0=f(x_0,y_0),$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nz - z_0 = (x-x_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0) + (y-y_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0).\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the surface \n\n$$\nz = 4-x^2-y^2.\n$$\n\nLet's find the equation of the tangent plane to the surface at the point $(0,\\sqrt{2},2),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/03/03df90a94615238e2126338f64d1f51fcefc379068a8cdc366b33b998cd69f45.png)\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y) = (0,\\sqrt{2})\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(0,\\sqrt{2}) & =-2(0)=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(0,\\sqrt{2}) & =-2(\\sqrt{2})=-2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-2 & =(x-0)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(0,\\sqrt{2})+(y-\\sqrt{2})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(0,\\sqrt{2}) \\\\ z-2 & =0(x-0)-2\\sqrt{2}(y-\\sqrt{2}) \\\\ z-2 & =-2\\sqrt{2}y+4 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2}y+z-6 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get that the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane is $2\\sqrt{2}y + z - 6 = 0.$\n\n![](images/d5/d51bdbe8820472334f7e1d942c027b6ad8164eaece58220f3f2e5cdb51544f05.png)" 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186134105 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=2 \\\\ {x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=2 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-8{x}_{2}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. If the general least-squares solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $3a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186134105 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=2 \\\\ {x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=2 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-8{x}_{2}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. If the general least-squares solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $3a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186147227 1071 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\ny'' + 16y = x\\sin(2x).\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions $y_1(x) = \\sin(4x)$ and $y_2(x) = \\cos(4x).$ \n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we seek a particular solution of the form\n![](images/28/284b6c87b050df7b5663c21dcf357b1f2fa66a86f6c28604ab6681267394ada1.png)\nwhere $u_1 = u_1(x), u_2 = u_2(x),$ that satisfies the constraint\n![](images/f6/f6f630f08ab3b335d43d12fa90c45df8fad0c73b83df793f43958e1ada7d199c.png)\n\nDifferentiating $y_p$ with respect to $x,$ we obtain\n\n![](images/32/32e3388da577fa240b722e46a266b47356239cc79821d7479ed4ea43bd4875d4.png)\n\nTherefore, we have the following constraint equation relating $u_1'$ and $u_2'{:}$\n\n![](images/28/2876b8d3c2d6920da03a828ab411bb489bda9743cc0a210486c725348e14f13c.png)" 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186147227 1071 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\ny'' + 16y = x\\sin(2x).\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions $y_1(x) = \\sin(4x)$ and $y_2(x) = \\cos(4x).$ \n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we seek a particular solution of the form\n![](images/c5/c5c61b7abb13d72afcf6227c4e6223c3fcb2d3ba4603887e019c707a58dc3e78.svg)\nwhere $u_1 = u_1(x), u_2 = u_2(x),$ that satisfies the constraint\n![](images/a4/a4767e062ae48669a097695597586b93fd3c8d40ae4b829295f161986c2622b1.svg)\n\nDifferentiating $y_p$ with respect to $x,$ we obtain\n\n![](images/71/71d5f83043daf3c2b8012b57f4519873361caa6a87414fcdd6a95bb477b4157c.svg)\n\nTherefore, we have the following constraint equation relating $u_1'$ and $u_2'{:}$\n\n![](images/98/98cd4d9d5d48d39b68ad528437417f4eb200ca988bec1199f6e4c473c0d76338.svg)" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186147228 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'' + 5y' - 6y = p(x),\n$$\n\nwhose corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\ny_1(x)=e^{x}, \\qquad y_2(x)=e^{-6x}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we seek a particular solution of the form\n![](images/2e/2e180f0d1ea06cd71caafeafb99628fdc8560253293ee5e9375dc84d5b735a64.png)\n\nwhere $u_1 = u_1(x), u_2 = u_2(x),$ that satisfies the constraint\n\n![](images/32/32e3388da577fa240b722e46a266b47356239cc79821d7479ed4ea43bd4875d4.png)\n\nSubstituting the expressions $y = y_p$ and $y' = y_p'$ into the differential equation yields the following relationship between $u_1', u