n}{AB},$ then we obtain a circular **segment**.*\"" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186175896 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504650 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186175896 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504675 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186175897 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504636 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186175897 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504670 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186176115 1050 420.0 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186176967 1064 "Let's consider yet more important parts of a circle:\n\n- The **circumference** of a circle is used to describe the perimeter of the circle, as shown in the left diagram below.\n\n- An **arc** is a part of the circumference which is enclosed between two points on a circle. In the right diagram below, we have the arc $\\overset{\\frown}{AB}.$\n\n![](images/5c/5c918b32007a68beecfdef486e9cd5d0818a2122ad672415608f761f7f7953e7.png)\n\nIf an arc is denoted using only two letters, we are referring to the *shortest* arc. However, to avoid ambiguity, we can use three letters. For example, in the diagram below, $\\overset{\\frown}{ACB}$ is the arc to the left and $\\overset{\\frown}{ADB}$ is the arc to the right.\n\n![](images/f6/f6329cc70c467e968628d2d82a0839253ee443881371893d6d7721724e9ae02a.png)" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186176967 1064 "Let's consider yet more important parts of a circle:\n\n- The **circumference** of a circle is used to describe the perimeter of the circle, as shown in the left diagram below.\n- An **arc** is a part of the circumference which is enclosed between two points on a circle. In the right diagram below, we have the arc $\\overset{\\frown}{AB}$.\n\n![](images/5c/5c918b32007a68beecfdef486e9cd5d0818a2122ad672415608f761f7f7953e7.png)\n\nIf an arc is denoted using only two letters, we are referring to the *shortest* arc. However, to avoid ambiguity, we can use three letters. For example, in the diagram below, $\\overset{\\frown}{ACB}$ is the arc to the left and $\\overset{\\frown}{ADB}$ is the arc to the right.\n\n![](images/f6/f6329cc70c467e968628d2d82a0839253ee443881371893d6d7721724e9ae02a.png)" 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186176974 1064 "Finally, we have two more elements of a circle to remember:\n\n- A **circular sector** is the region bounded by an arc of the circle and two radii which connect the center of the circle with the endpoints of the arc. In the left diagram below, we have the circular sector $OCD$.\n- A **circular segment** is the region bounded by an arc of the circle and the chord that connects the endpoints of the arc. In the right diagram below, we have the circular segment $PQ$.\n\n![](images/84/848e64be2b80b78d88da42473f6778d4012f7ec52ae6950f4ca3635b5b154ca6.png)" 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186176974 1064 "Finally, we have two more elements of a circle to remember:\n\n- A **circular sector** is the region bounded by an arc of the circle and two radii which connect the center of the circle with the endpoints of the arc. In the left diagram below, we have the circular sector $OCD.$\n\n- A **circular segment** is the region bounded by an arc of the circle and the chord that connects the endpoints of the arc. In the right diagram below, we have the circular segment $PQ.$\n\n![](images/84/848e64be2b80b78d88da42473f6778d4012f7ec52ae6950f4ca3635b5b154ca6.png)" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186178954 1078 #edb/ref 17592186178958 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186179817 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504610 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186179817 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504645 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186179818 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504652 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186179889 1050 420.0 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186180235 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186180235 1074 "We are given that the ratio of red roses to the total number of roses is $\\frac{3}{10}.$\n\nIn this fraction, the numerator represents the number of red roses and the denominator represents the number of  total number of roses.\n\nWe can make a denominator of $90$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $9:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{3\\times 9}{10\\times 9}=\\frac{27}{90}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there are $27$ red roses in the garden. Hence, the number of white roses is\n\n$$\n90-27=63.\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186180636 1061 7795 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186180636 1061 637169 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186180637 1061 7796 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186180637 1061 637170 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186180638 1061 7797 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186180638 1061 637173 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186184238 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504613 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186184238 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504615 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186184238 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504643 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186184238 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504648 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186184239 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504686 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186184239 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504689 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186184240 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504629 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186184240 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504644 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186184240 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504684 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186184398 1050 540.0 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186185359 1064 "In this lesson, we will learn how to deal with limits involving inverse trigonometric functions. \n\nLet's begin by recalling the graph of $y=\\arcsin(x).$\n\n![](images/df/df22eea4331c47da3047381ca672fd80ebf75118054316fe88c0e2d96dae0d34.png)\n\nUsing the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The domain of $\\arcsin(x)$ is $x \\in [-1,1].$\n\n- For every value $a \\in (-1,1),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to a} \\arcsin(x) = \\arcsin(a).\n$$\n\n \nIn other words, we can evaluate the limit using direct substitution. For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1/2}\\operatorname{arcsin}(x) & =\\operatorname{arcsin}(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For any value $a$ that does *not* lie in the domain of $\\arcsin(x),$ the limit\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to a} \\arcsin(x)\n$$\n\n \ndoes *not* exist.\n\n- At the point $x=-1,$ the limit does *not* exist because the left-sided limit does not exist. However, the right-sided limit *does* exist and is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}\\operatorname{arcsin}(x) & =\\operatorname{arcsin}(-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Similarly, at the point $x=1,$ the limit does *not* exist because the right-sided limit does not exist. However, the left-sided limit *does* exist and is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\operatorname{arcsin}(x) & =\\operatorname{arcsin}(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit properties of $y=\\arccos(x)$ are similar. Let's discuss them briefly." 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186185359 1064 "In this lesson, we will learn how to deal with limits involving inverse trigonometric functions. \n\nLet's begin by recalling the graph of $y=\\arcsin(x).$\n\n![](images/df/df22eea4331c47da3047381ca672fd80ebf75118054316fe88c0e2d96dae0d34.png)\n\nUsing the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The domain of $\\arcsin(x)$ is $x \\in [-1,1].$\n\n- For every value $a \\in (-1,1),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to a} \\arcsin(x) = \\arcsin(a).\n$$\n\n \nIn other words, we can evaluate the limit using direct substitution. For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1/2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x) & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For any value $a$ that does *not* lie in the domain of $\\arcsin(x),$ the limit\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to a} \\arcsin(x)\n$$\n\n \ndoes *not* exist.\n\n- At the point $x=-1,$ the limit does *not* exist because the left-sided limit does not exist. However, the right-sided limit *does* exist and is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x) & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Similarly, at the point $x=1,$ the limit does *not* exist because the right-sided limit does not exist. However, the left-sided limit *does* exist and is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x) & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit properties of $y=\\arccos(x)$ are similar. Let's discuss them briefly." 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186185360 1064 "We can deduce limits at infinity of transformed inverse trigonometric functions by sketching their graphs.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\, \\infty} \\dfrac{2}{\\pi}\\arctan \\left(x-\\pi\\right)\n$$\n\nTo evaluate this limit, we first sketch the graph of  $y =\\arctan \\left(x-\\pi\\right){:}$ \n\n![](images/51/51a0b9a210147b3791ce20a2f65320e20618826671ea6c7c31a27f8647595079.png)\n\nThe domain of   $\\arctan \\left(x-\\pi\\right)$ is $(-\\infty,\\infty).$   Moreover, from the graph, we see that \n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to \\infty} \\arctan \\left(x-\\pi\\right)=\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} }  .\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the algebra of limits, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\operatorname{arctan}(x-\\pi ) & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}(x-\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLimits at infinity involving the inverse sine and cosine functions usually do not exist. Let's see an example." 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186185360 1064 "We can deduce limits at infinity of transformed inverse trigonometric functions by sketching their graphs.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\, \\infty} \\dfrac{2}{\\pi}\\arctan \\left(x-\\pi\\right)\n$$\n\nTo evaluate this limit, we first sketch the graph of  $y =\\arctan \\left(x-\\pi\\right){:}$  \n\n![](images/51/51a0b9a210147b3791ce20a2f65320e20618826671ea6c7c31a27f8647595079.png)\n\nThe domain of   $\\arctan \\left(x-\\pi\\right)$ is $(-\\infty,\\infty).$   Moreover, from the graph, we see that \n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to \\infty} \\arctan \\left(x-\\pi\\right)=\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} }  .\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the algebra of limits, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x-\\pi ) & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x-\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLimits at infinity involving the inverse sine and cosine functions usually do not exist. Let's see an example." 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186185362 1064 "The graph of $y=\\arctan x$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/fc/fc06a4e63f38a315dd3944f65f871cca2dbdddee26ec268e857f79e5f4ab8848.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The domain is $x \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\n- For every value $a,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to a} \\arctan(x) = \\arctan(a).\n$$\n\n- The limit as $x\\to-\\infty$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}(x) & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The limit as $x\\to\\infty$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}(x) & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use the algebra of limits to evaluate expressions involving inverse trigonometric functions. Let's see some examples." 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186185362 1064 "The graph of $y=\\arctan x$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/fc/fc06a4e63f38a315dd3944f65f871cca2dbdddee26ec268e857f79e5f4ab8848.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The domain is $x \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\n- For every value $a,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to a} \\arctan(x) = \\arctan(a).\n$$\n\n- The limit as $x\\to-\\infty$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x) & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The limit as $x\\to\\infty$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x) & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use the algebra of limits to evaluate expressions involving inverse trigonometric functions. Let's see some examples." 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186185363 1064 "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\arccos(x).$\n\n![](images/9d/9dcc5a116c2a564547283783a7df7b1cd3a9ab3b76ac24dc2695c4cb367796d5.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The domain is $x \\in [-1,1].$\n\n- For every value $a \\in (-1,1),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to a} \\arccos(x) = \\arccos(a).\n$$\n\n- For any value $a$ that does *not* lie in the domain of $\\arccos(x),$ the limit\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to a} \\arccos(x)\n$$\n\n \ndoes *not* exist.\n\n- The right-sided limit at $x=-1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}\\operatorname{arccos}(x) & =\\operatorname{arccos}(-1) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Similarly, the left-sided limit at $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\operatorname{arccos}(x) & =\\operatorname{arccos}(1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's deduce the limit properties of $y = \\arctan x.$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186185363 1064 "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\arccos(x).$\n\n![](images/9d/9dcc5a116c2a564547283783a7df7b1cd3a9ab3b76ac24dc2695c4cb367796d5.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The domain is $x \\in [-1,1].$\n\n- For every value $a \\in (-1,1),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to a} \\arccos(x) = \\arccos(a).\n$$\n\n- For any value $a$ that does *not* lie in the domain of $\\arccos(x),$ the limit\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to a} \\arccos(x)\n$$\n\n \ndoes *not* exist.\n\n- The right-sided limit at $x=-1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x) & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-1) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Similarly, the left-sided limit at $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x) & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's deduce the limit properties of $y = \\arctan x.$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186187776 1075 #edb/ref 17592186187777 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186187776 1075 #edb/ref 17592186187778 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186187776 1075 #edb/ref 17592186187779 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186187776 1075 #edb/ref 17592186187780 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186187776 1075 #edb/ref 17592186187781 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186187776 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504177 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186187776 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504238 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186187776 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504426 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186187776 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504458 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186187776 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504462 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186187776 1078 #edb/ref 17592186504458 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186188374 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504669 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186188374 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504687 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186188950 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186188950 1071 "$5-3(x+1)=$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186188950 1071 "What is $5 - 3(x + 1)$?" 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186188950 1074 "Notice that the expression we need to subtract from $5$ must be expanded using the distributive law. \n\nSo first, let's write some big brackets around the expression in parentheses, keeping the minus sign *outside*.\n\n$$\n5-[3(x+1)]\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the expression inside the brackets using the distributive law. This gives\n\n$$\n5-[3x+3].\n$$\n\nNow, we remove the brackets by distributing the negative sign. This gives\n\n$$\n5-3x-3.\n$$\n\nFinally, we collect like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5-3x-3 & = \\\\ (5-3)-3x & = \\\\ 2-3x & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186189604 1075 #edb/ref 17592186189605 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186189604 1075 #edb/ref 17592186189606 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186189604 1075 #edb/ref 17592186189607 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186189604 1075 #edb/ref 17592186189608 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186189604 1075 #edb/ref 17592186189609 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186189604 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504050 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186189604 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504053 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186189604 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504062 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186189604 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504336 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186189604 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504440 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186189604 1078 #edb/ref 17592186504053 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186192539 1071 "Solve the inequality $10x - 4 > 16$." 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186192539 1071 "Solve the inequality $10x - 4 > 16.$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186192539 1074 "First, we apply the addition principle, adding $4$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10x-4 & >16 \\\\ 10x-4+4 & >16+4 \\\\ 10x+0 & >20 \\\\ 10x & >20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSecond, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by $10{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10x & >20 \\\\ \\frac{10x}{10} & >\\frac{20}{10} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{10}x}{\\cancel{10}} & >2 \\\\ x & >2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x> 2.$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186192539 1074 "First, we apply the addition principle, adding $4$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}10x - 4 & > 16 \\\\ 10x - 4 + 4 & > 16 + 4 \\\\ 10x + 0 & > 20 \\\\ 10x & > 20\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSecond, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by $10{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}10x & > 20 \\\\ \\frac{10x}{10} & > \\frac{20}{10} \\\\ \\frac{10x}{10} & > 2 \\\\ x & > 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x> 2$." 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186192540 1071 "Solve the inequality $-2x + 3  > -1.$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186192540 1071 "Solve the inequality $-2x + 3 > -1$." 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186192540 1074 "First, we apply the addition principle, subtracting $3$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+3 & >-1 \\\\ -2x+3-3 & >-1-3 \\\\ -2x+0 & >-4 \\\\ -2x & >-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by $-2.$ \n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x\\,\\, & >\\,\\,-4 \\\\ \\frac{-2x}{-2}\\, & <\\,\\,\\frac{-4}{-2} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{-2}x}{\\cancel{-2}}\\, & <\\,\\,2 \\\\ x\\,\\, & <\\,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x<2.$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186192540 1074 "First, we apply the addition principle, subtracting $3$ from both sides:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-2x + 3 & > - 1 \\\\ -2x + 3 - 3 & > - 1 - 3 \\\\ -2x + 0 & > - 4 \\\\ -2x & > - 4\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThen, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by $-2$.\n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2x & > - 4 \\\\ \\frac{-2x}{-2} & <\\frac{-4}{-2} \\\\ \\frac{-2x}{-2} & <2 \\\\ x & <2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x<2$." 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186192541 1071 "Solve the inequality $-11 \\le     -8y + 5.$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186192541 1071 "Solve the inequality $-11 \\le -8y + 5$." 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186192541 1074 "First, we apply the addition principle, subtracting $5$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-11 & \\le -8y+5 \\\\ -11-5 & \\le -8y+5-5 \\\\ -16 & \\le -8y+0 \\\\ -16 & \\le -8y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by $-8.$ \n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-16\\,\\, & \\le \\,\\,-8y \\\\ \\frac{-16}{-8}\\, & \\ge \\,\\,\\frac{-8y}{-8} \\\\ 2\\, & \\ge \\,\\,\\frac{\\cancel{-8}y}{\\cancel{-8}} \\\\ 2\\, & \\ge \\,\\,y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's swap the left and right-hand sides so that the variable is on the left-hand side. Remember, we also need to flip the inequality.\n\n$$\ny \\le   2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y \\le   2.$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186192541 1074 "First, we apply the addition principle, subtracting $5$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-11 & \\le - 8y + 5 \\\\ -11 - 5 & \\le - 8y + 5 - 5 \\\\ -16 & \\le - 8y + 0 \\\\ -16 & \\le - 8y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by $-8$.\n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-16 & \\le- 8y \\\\ \\frac{-16}{-8} & \\ge\\frac{-8y}{-8} \\\\ 2 & \\ge\\frac{-8y}{-8} \\\\ 2 & \\gey\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's swap the left and right-hand sides so that the variable is on the left-hand side. Remember, we also need to flip the inequality.\n\n$$\ny \\le 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny \\le 2\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186192581 1074 "The inequality\n\n$$\nx \\geq 1\n$$\n\ncorresponds to all numbers greater than or equal to $1$. A number line for this inequality is shown below:\n\n![](images/82/829c0bd7c365ef7e5b1d3bee70a632b34cd644349a7528b688abdfd57575a1ab.png)\n\nFrom the segment on the number line, we have the following:\n\n- The left endpoint $1$ *is* included in the interval.\n- There is no right endpoint - it continues forever. So, we write the interval with infinity as the right endpoint, not included.\n\nTherefore, using interval notation, we represent\n\n$$\nx \\geq 1\n$$\n\nas\n\n$$\nx \\in [1,\\infty)\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186192581 1074 "The inequality $x \\geq 1$ corresponds to all numbers greater than or equal to $1.$ A number line for this inequality is shown below:\n\n![](images/82/829c0bd7c365ef7e5b1d3bee70a632b34cd644349a7528b688abdfd57575a1ab.png)\n\nFrom the segment on the number line, we have the following:\n\n- The left endpoint $1$ *is* included in the interval.\n\n- There is no right endpoint - it continues forever. So, we write the interval with infinity as the right endpoint, not included.\n\nTherefore, using interval notation, we represent $x \\geq 1$ as\n\n$$\nx \\in [1,\\infty).\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186192582 1071 "What inequality corresponds the interval $x\\in\\left[5, \\infty\\right)$?" 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186192582 1071 "What inequality corresponds the interval $x\\in\\left[5, \\infty\\right)?$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186192582 1074 "The statement\n\n$$\nx\\in\\left[5, \\infty\\right)\n$$\n\nmeans that $x$ lies between $5$ and $\\infty$, where\n\n- the left endpoint $5$ *is* included, and\n- the right endpoint $\\infty$ is *not* included.\n\nTherefore, the inequality represented by this interval is\n\n$$\n5 \\leq x < \\infty\n$$\n\nHowever, since all numbers are smaller than infinity, it suffices to write\n\n$$\n5 \\leq x\n$$\n\nor, equivalently,\n\n$$\nx \\geq 5\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186192582 1074 "The statement $x\\in\\left[5, \\infty\\right)$ means that $x$ lies between $5$ and $\\infty,$ where \n\n- the left endpoint $5$ *is* included, and\n\n- the right endpoint $\\infty$ is *not* included.\n\nTherefore, the inequality represented by this interval is \n\n$$\n5 \\leq   x <     \\infty.\n$$\n\nHowever, since all numbers are smaller than infinity, it suffices to write \n\n$$\n5 \\leq   x\n$$\n\nor, equivalently, \n\n$$\nx \\geq 5.\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186192583 1074 "The inequality\n\n$$\nx \\leq 1\n$$\n\ncorresponds to all numbers less than or equal to $1$. A number line for this interval is shown below:\n\n![](images/b5/b506caa666e4715b3f153459b8ec7f650ff53e97401ec9887fe6c294fb680887.png)\n\nFrom the segment on the number line, we have the following:\n\n- There is no left endpoint - it continues forever. So, we write the interval with negative infinity as the left endpoint, not included.\n- The right endpoint $1$ *is* included in the interval.\n\nTherefore, using interval notation, we represent\n\n$$\nx \\leq 1\n$$\n\nas\n\n$$\nx\\in(-\\infty, 1]\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186192583 1074 "The inequality $x \\leq 1$ corresponds to all numbers less than or equal to $1.$ A number line for this interval is shown below:\n\n![](images/b5/b506caa666e4715b3f153459b8ec7f650ff53e97401ec9887fe6c294fb680887.png)\n\nFrom the segment on the number line, we have the following:\n\n- There is no left endpoint - it continues forever. So, we write the interval with negative infinity as the left endpoint, not included.\n\n- The right endpoint $1$ *is* included in the interval.\n\nTherefore, using interval notation, we represent $x \\leq 1$ as\n\n$$\nx\\in(-\\infty, 1].\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186192584 1071 "What inequality corresponds to the interval $x\\in\\left(-\\infty, 9\\right)$?" 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186192584 1071 "What inequality corresponds to the interval $x\\in\\left(-\\infty, 9\\right)?$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186192584 1074 "The statement\n\n$$\nx \\in \\left(-\\infty, 9\\right)\n$$\n\nmeans that $x$ lies between $-\\infty$ and $9$, where\n\n- the left endpoint $-\\infty$ is *not* included, and\n- the right endpoint $9$ is *not* included.\n\nTherefore, the inequality represented by this interval is\n$-\\infty < x < 9$.\n\nHowever, since all numbers are greater than negative infinity, it suffices to write\n$x < 9$." 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186192584 1074 "The statement $x \\in \\left(-\\infty, 9\\right)$ means that $x$ lies between $-\\infty$ and $9,$ where \n\n- the left endpoint $-\\infty$ is *not* included, and\n\n- the right endpoint $9$ is *not* included.\n\nTherefore, the inequality represented by this interval is \n\n$$\n-\\infty <   x <   9.\n$$\n\nHowever, since all numbers are greater than negative infinity, it suffices to write \n\n$$\nx <   9.\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186192627 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504649 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186192627 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504660 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186192628 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504602 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186192628 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504697 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186192629 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504605 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186192629 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504642 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186192630 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504640 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186192630 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504681 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186192842 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504668 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186192842 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504704 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186192843 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504604 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186192843 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504662 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186192844 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504639 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186192844 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504661 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186192903 1050 420.0 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186192906 1050 420.0 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186192953 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186192953 1071 "The inequality $x < - 7$ can be expressed in interval notation as" 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186192953 1071 "The inequality $x<-7$ can be expressed in interval notation as" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186192953 1074 "The inequality $x<-7$ corresponds to all numbers less than $-7.$ A number line for this interval is shown below:\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/30/309a19968d017e575543f8165405dea2fe102a2d9a10d575ecf1cead17d36e07.png)\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFrom the segment on the number line, we have the following:\n\n- There is no left endpoint - it continues forever. So, we write the interval with negative infinity as the left endpoint, not included.\n\n- The right endpoint $-7$ is *not* included in the interval.\n\nTherefore, using interval notation, we represent $x<-7$ as\n\n$$\nx\\in (-\\infty ,-7).\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186193912 1064 "Suppose that we wish to find the solution to the inequality\n\n$$\n2x + 6 < 12\n$$\n\nAn inequality like this one can be solved just like an equation, using the addition and multiplication principles.\n\nFirst, we apply the addition principle, subtracting $6$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2x + 6 & < 12 \\\\ 2x + 6 - 6 & < 12 - 6 \\\\ 2x + 0 & < 6 \\\\ 2x & < 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSecond, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by $2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2x & < 6 \\\\ \\frac{2x}{2} & < \\frac{6}{2} \\\\ \\frac{2x}{2} & < 3 \\\\ x & < 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe solution is $x < 3$ In other words, any value less than $3$ satisfies the inequality.\n\nLet's see another example." 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186193912 1064 "Suppose that we wish to find the solution to the inequality\n\n$$\n2x + 6 < 12.\n$$\n\nAn inequality like this one can be solved just like an equation, using the addition and multiplication principles.\n\nFirst, we apply the addition principle, subtracting $6$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+6 & <12 \\\\ 2x+6-6 & <12-6 \\\\ 2x+0 & <6 \\\\ 2x & <6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSecond, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by $2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & <6 \\\\ \\frac{2x}{2} & <\\frac{6}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{2}x}{\\cancel{2}} & <3 \\\\ x & <3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution is $x < 3$ In other words, any value less than $3$ satisfies the inequality.\n\nLet's see another example." 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186193939 1064 "Consider the following inequality:\n\n$$\nx > 0\n$$\n\nWe can represent this inequality using a number line as follows:\n\n![](images/79/79664d125db14d5b8534d592d232839659a6d0efbd527379207b50d71f9e7454.png)\n\nHow do we represent this inequality using interval notation?\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The left endpoint $x=0$ is *not* included. Therefore, we use a parenthesis $($ for this endpoint.\n- The interval continues forever in the positive direction. To denote this, we must introduce the **infinity** symbol $\\infty$. Infinity is not a number but a concept that means *larger than any number*.\n- We use a right parenthesis $)$ in conjunction with the $\\infty$ symbol.\n\nTherefore, we can express the inequality $x > 0$ in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\nx \\in (0, \\infty)\n$$\n\n**Watch Out!** Whenever $\\infty$ appears in an interval, it is *always* associated with a right parenthesis. Infinity is *never* associated with a bracket, even if the other endpoint is." 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186193939 1064 "Consider the following inequality: \n\n$$\nx > 0\n$$\n\nWe can represent this inequality using a number line as follows:\n\n![](images/79/79664d125db14d5b8534d592d232839659a6d0efbd527379207b50d71f9e7454.png)\n\nHow do we represent this inequality using interval notation?\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The left endpoint $x=0$ is *not* included. Therefore, we use a parenthesis $($ for this endpoint.\n\n- The interval continues forever in the positive direction. To denote this, we must introduce the **infinity** symbol $\\infty.$ Infinity is not a number but a concept that means *larger than any number*.\n\n- We use a right parenthesis $)$ in conjunction with the $\\infty$ symbol.\n\nTherefore, we can express the inequality $x > 0$ in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\nx \\in (0, \\infty)\n$$\n\n**Watch Out!** Whenever $\\infty$ appears in an interval, it is *always* associated with a right parenthesis. Infinity is *never* associated with a bracket, even if the other endpoint is." 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186193940 1064 "Now, consider the following inequality:\n\n$$\nx \\lt 0\n$$\n\nWe can represent this inequality using a number line as follows:\n\n![](images/52/52be4b1ae3d32f2d3bb1dbf9f4faa1f288cff5736870064533b958ac89a76dad.png)\n\nTo represent this inequality using interval notation, we note the following:\n\n- The right endpoint $x=0$ is *not* included. Therefore, we use a parenthesis $)$ for this endpoint.\n- The interval continues forever in the negative direction. To denote this, we use the **negative infinity** symbol $-\\infty$. We use a left parenthesis $($ in conjunction with the negative infinity symbol.\n\nTherefore, we can express the inequality $x < 0$ in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\nx \\in (-\\infty, 0)\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186193940 1064 "Now, consider the following inequality: \n\n$$\nx \\lt 0\n$$\n\nWe can represent this inequality using a number line as follows:\n\n![](images/52/52be4b1ae3d32f2d3bb1dbf9f4faa1f288cff5736870064533b958ac89a76dad.png)\n\nTo represent this inequality using interval notation, we note the following:\n\n- The right endpoint $x=0$ is *not* included. Therefore, we use a parenthesis $)$ for this endpoint.\n\n- The interval continues forever in the negative direction. To denote this, we use the **negative infinity** symbol $-\\infty.$ We use a left parenthesis $($ in conjunction with the negative infinity symbol.\n\nTherefore, we can express the inequality $x < 0$ in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\nx \\in (-\\infty, 0)\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186233889 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}\\frac{25\\operatorname{arccos}(x)}{{x}^{2}+\\pi }=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186233889 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}\\frac{25\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)}{{x}^{2}+\\pi }=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186233889 1074 "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}(x):$\n\n![](images/85/85a182aabfe64c27eaafde94e3bdbede9409b02cfd87395cb2fc5543b09c4e05.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arccos}(x)$ is $x\\in [-1,1].$ \n\nNow, substituting $x=1$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}\\frac{25\\operatorname{arccos}(x)}{{x}^{2}+\\pi } & =\\frac{25\\operatorname{arccos}(1)}{{1}^{2}+\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{25\\cdot 0}{1+\\pi } \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186233889 1074 "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x):$\n\n![](images/85/85a182aabfe64c27eaafde94e3bdbede9409b02cfd87395cb2fc5543b09c4e05.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in [-1,1].$  \n\nNow, substituting $x=1$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}\\frac{25\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)}{{x}^{2}+\\pi } & =\\frac{25\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(1)}{{1}^{2}+\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{25\\cdot 0}{1+\\pi } \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186233940 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }\\frac{5}{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{arctan}(2x+7))=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186233940 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }\\frac{5}{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+7))=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186233940 1074 "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}(2x+7):$\n\n![](images/37/371cbb841c67494626038fc93747a1d0a2b2245f422364911f1a859f4711868d.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}(2x+7)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Moreover, from the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}(2x+7)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }\\frac{5}{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{arctan}(2x+7))=\\frac{5}{2\\pi }\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=-\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186233940 1074 "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+7):$\n\n![](images/37/371cbb841c67494626038fc93747a1d0a2b2245f422364911f1a859f4711868d.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+7)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Moreover, from the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+7)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }\\frac{5}{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+7))=\\frac{5}{2\\pi }\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=-\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186233953 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\operatorname{arctan}(x+1))=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186233953 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+1))=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186233953 1074 "First, let's sketch the graph