 we're given that the lower limit $a=-\\pi .$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{\\pi }{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{n}=\\frac{b-(-\\pi )}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=0.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=-\\pi +\\frac{\\pi k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})={cos}^{2}{x}_{k}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}{cos}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[{cos}^{2}(-\\pi +\\frac{\\pi k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)={cos}^{2}x$ and $b=0.$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186234108 1074 "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=-\\pi .$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{\\pi }{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{n}=\\frac{b-(-\\pi )}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=0.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=-\\pi +\\frac{\\pi k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})={cos}^{2}⁡{x}_{k}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}{cos}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[{cos}^{2}⁡(-\\pi +\\frac{\\pi k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)={cos}^{2}⁡x$ and $b=0.$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186311879 1071 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+12y=g(x).\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-3x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-4x}:$\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={{field-1}}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}={u}_{1}(x),{u}_{2}={u}_{2}(x),$ that satisfies the constraint\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}(x)={{field-2}}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expressions $y={y}_{p}$ and ${y}^{′}={y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation yields the following relationship between ${u}_{1}^{′},{u}_{2}^{′},$ and $g(x):$\n\n$$\n{{field-3}}\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186311879 1071 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+12y=g(x).\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-3x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-4x}:$According to the method of variation of parameters, we seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={{field-1}}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}={u}_{1}(x),{u}_{2}={u}_{2}(x),$ that satisfies the constraint\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}(x)={{field-2}}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expressions $y={y}_{p}$ and ${y}^{′}={y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation yields the following relationship between ${u}_{1}^{′},{u}_{2}^{′},$ and $g(x):$\n\n$$\n{{field-3}}\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 false] [17592186311934 1071 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-9y={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-3x}.$ \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that ${u}_{1}(x)=\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ find the function $g.$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186311934 1071 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-9y={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-3x}.$ \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{alig