504599 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504407 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504599 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504427 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504599 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504599 1077 "selection" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504599 1078 #edb/ref 17592186504427 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504599 1079 #uuid "f8cc4165-06fc-5353-97db-60ce6554a423" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504600 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504041 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504600 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504119 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504600 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504309 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504600 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504321 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504600 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504478 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504600 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504600 1077 "selection" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504600 1078 #edb/ref 17592186504119 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504600 1079 #uuid "fe2b2c06-359a-556f-b20c-5c316319fefe" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504601 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504556 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504601 1069 "q-342622" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504601 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504601 1071 "The function $h(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nh(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-2}{\\sqrt{x+a}-\\sqrt{2+a}}, & x\\ne 2, \\\\ 6, & x=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=2,$ what is the value of $a?$\n\n$a=$ {{field-1}}" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504601 1072 #uuid "0241cfe8-3868-5b51-b660-c610e52d9bb3" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504601 1074 "To calculate the limit, we multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}h(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{x-2}{\\sqrt{x+a}-\\sqrt{2+a}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(\\frac{x-2}{\\sqrt{x+a}-\\sqrt{2+a}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{x+a}+\\sqrt{2+a}}{\\sqrt{x+a}+\\sqrt{2+a}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{(x-2)(\\sqrt{x+a}+\\sqrt{2+a})}{(\\sqrt{x+a})^{2}-(\\sqrt{2+a})^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{(x-2)(\\sqrt{x+a}+\\sqrt{2+a})}{x-2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{\\cancel{(x-2)}(\\sqrt{x+a}+\\sqrt{2+a})}{\\cancel{x-2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(\\sqrt{x+a}+\\sqrt{2+a}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2+a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $h(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}h(x) & =h(2) \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2+a} & =6 \\\\ \\sqrt{2+a} & =3 \\\\ 2+a & =9 \\\\ a & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting $a=7$ removes the point discontinuity." 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504602 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504492 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504602 1069 "q-154781" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504602 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504602 1071 "The interval $x\\in (-14,\\infty )$ corresponds to the inequality" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504602 1072 #uuid "05484b4a-57a2-55c2-9049-f423ce09e86a" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504602 1074 "The statement $x\\in (-14,\\infty )$ means that $x$ lies between $-14$ and $\\infty ,$ where \n\n- the left endpoint $-14$ is *not* included, and\n\n- the right endpoint $\\infty $ is *not* included.\n\nTherefore, the inequality represented by this interval is \n\n$$\n-14<x<\\infty .\n$$\n\nHowever, since all numbers are smaller than infinity, it suffices to write \n\n$$\n-14<x\n$$\n\nor, equivalently, \n\n$$\nx>-14.\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504603 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504588 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504603 1069 "q-342552" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504603 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504603 1071 "The function $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{log}_{5}⁡(2x+k), & x\\ne -1, \\\\ 3, & x=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1,$ what is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504603 1072 #uuid "060bce41-c046-5d08-898b-cfb8261e7661" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504603 1074 "From the definition of $f(x),$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}{log}_{5}⁡(2x+k) \\\\  & ={log}_{5}⁡(k-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-1,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x) & =f(-1) \\\\ {log}_{5}⁡(k-2) & =3 \\\\ k-2 & ={5}^{3} \\\\ k-2 & =125 \\\\ k & =127.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting $k=127$ removes the point discontinuity." 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504604 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504521 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504604 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504551 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504604 1069 "q-177680" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504604 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504604 1071 "The solution to the inequality $-7t+2\\le -5$ is $t${{field-1}}{{field-2}}." 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504604 1072 #uuid "0822285c-ebb1-578e-9679-e7a486cbcc58" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504604 1074 "First, we apply the addition principle, subtracting $2$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7t+2 & \\le -5 \\\\ -7t+2-2 & \\le -5-2 \\\\ -7t+0 & \\le -7 \\\\ -7t & \\le -7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by $-7.$ \n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7t\\,\\, & \\le \\,\\,-7 \\\\ \\frac{-7t}{-7}\\, & \\ge \\,\\,\\frac{-7}{-7} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{-7}t}{\\cancel{-7}}\\, & \\ge \\,\\,1 \\\\ t\\,\\, & \\ge \\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $t\\ge 1.$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504605 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504542 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504605 1069 "q-1240" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504605 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504605 1071 "The inequality $x<5$ can be expressed in interval notation as" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504605 1072 #uuid "0b34c9dd-15c6-517b-a545-622b3797f82a" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504605 1074 "The inequality $x<5$ corresponds to all numbers less than $5.$ A number line for this interval is shown below:\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/61/61a3fcdcc19f822c0d94b806441ea2888dab3bb9ac0b846c965a8fe23ad078ae.png)\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFrom the segment on the number line, we have the following:\n\n- There is no left endpoint - it continues forever. So, we write the interval with negative infinity as the left endpoint, not included.\n\n- The right endpoint $5$ is *not* included in the interval.\n\nTherefore, using interval notation, we represent $x<5$ as\n\n$$\nx\\in (-\\infty ,5).\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504606 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504503 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504606 1069 "q-48574" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504606 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504606 1071 "The function $g(x)$ is given by \n\n$$\ng(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+1}-\\sqrt{1-x}}{ax}, & x\\ne 0, \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}, & x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor what value of $a$ is the function continuous at $x=0?$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504606 1072 #uuid "0d67e759-1525-5679-9c05-4036359d7add" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504606 1074 "To calculate the limit, we multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+1}-\\sqrt{1-x}}{ax} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{\\sqrt{x+1}-\\sqrt{1-x}}{ax}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{x+1}+\\sqrt{1-x}}{\\sqrt{x+1}+\\sqrt{1-x}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x+1})^{2}-(\\sqrt{1-x})^{2}}{ax(\\sqrt{x+1}+\\sqrt{1-x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2x}{ax(\\sqrt{x+1}+\\sqrt{1-x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2\\cancel{x}}{a\\cancel{x}(\\sqrt{x+1}+\\sqrt{1-x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2}{a(\\sqrt{x+1}+\\sqrt{1-x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{a(\\sqrt{0+1}+\\sqrt{1-0})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}g(x) & =g(0) \\\\ \\frac{1}{a} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ a & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting $a=4$ removes the point discontinuity." 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504607 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504531 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504607 1069 "q-342648" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504607 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504607 1071 "The function $g(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x-b}-\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}-b}}, & x\\ne 0, \\\\ 10, & x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor what value of $b$ is the function continuous at $x=0?$\n\n$b=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504607 1072 #uuid "1326dade-f2c7-5d3e-a020-cedd74fc345f" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504607 1074 "To calculate the limit, we multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x-b}-\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}-b}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x-b}-\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}-b}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{x-b}+\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}-b}}{\\sqrt{x-b}+\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}-b}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(\\sqrt{x-b}+\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}-b})}{(\\sqrt{x-b})^{2}-(\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}-b})^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(\\sqrt{x-b}+\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}-b})}{x-2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(\\sqrt{x-b}+\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}-b})}{x(1-2x)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\cancel{x}(\\sqrt{x-b}+\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}-b})}{\\cancel{x}(1-2x)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\sqrt{x-b}+\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}-b}}{1-2x} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{-b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}g(x) & =g(0) \\\\ 2\\sqrt{-b} & =10 \\\\ \\sqrt{-b} & =5 \\\\ -b & =25 \\\\ b & =-25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting $b=-25$ removes the point discontinuity." 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504608 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504511 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504608 1069 "q-125530" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504608 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504608 1071 "Find the $72.9$th percentile of the random variable $X$ if the probability distribution function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{16}\\sqrt{x},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 4, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504608 1072 #uuid "158dc85d-a65b-564b-ba7e-be30418efec3" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504608 1074 "The $100p$th percentile of a random variable $X$ is the number ${x}_{p}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le {x}_{p})=p.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we want to find the $72.9$th percentile, which corresponds to the decimal $p=0.729.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {x}_{0.729}) & ={\\int }_{0}^{{x}_{0.729}}\\frac{3}{16}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{8}|}_{0}^{{x}_{0.729}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{0.729}^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $72.9$th percentile is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}_{0.729}^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{8} & =0.729 \\\\ {x}_{0.729}^{\\frac{1}{2}} & =\\sqrt[3]{(8)(0.729)} \\\\ {x}_{0.729}^{\\frac{1}{2}} & =(2)(0.9) \\\\ {x}_{0.729} & =(1.8)^{2} \\\\ {x}_{0.729} & =3.24.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504609 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504546 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504609 1069 "q-346221" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504609 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504609 1071 "What is ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\cdot \\,\\frac{\\pi }{6n}$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504609 1072 #uuid "174ba687-7b9b-5ccb-9e0f-e16e772aa04c" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504609 1074 "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),$ the lower bound is $a=0$ and the upper bound is $b=\\pi /6.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{\\pi /6-0}{n}=\\frac{\\pi }{6n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f(0+i\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f(\\frac{\\pi i}{6n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi i}{6n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi i}{2n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504610 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504529 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504610 1069 "q-147074" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504610 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504610 1071 "A coin was tossed multiple times.\nThe ratio of times when the coin landed heads up to the total number of tosses is $4:9.$\nIf the coin landed heads up $16$ times, how many times did it land tails up?" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504610 1072 #uuid "1e6db99f-016f-5b3d-be15-91b5ecbdbcbd" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504610 1074 "We start with the ratio $4:9$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{9}\n$$\n\nIn this fraction, the numerator represents the number of heads, and the denominator represents the total number of tosses.\n\nWe can make a numerator of $16$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $4:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{4\\times 4}{9\\times 4}=\\frac{16}{36}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coin was tossed $36$ times in total. \nSo, the number of tails is\n\n$$\n36-16=20.\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504611 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504580 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504611 1069 "q-107916" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504611 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504611 1071 "The function $f(x)$ is defined for every real number $x$ and is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{cx+4}, & x\\ne -1, \\\\ \\frac{1}{27}, & x=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1,$ what is the value of $c?$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504611 1072 #uuid "215b8d43-0fa8-5bc5-9a2a-4cbd17fc3abb" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504611 1074 "From the definition of $f(x),$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{cx+4}={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{4-c}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-1,$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x) & =f(-1) \\\\ {(\\frac{1}{3})}^{4-c} & =\\frac{1}{27} \\\\ {(\\frac{1}{3})}^{4-c} & ={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{3} \\\\ 4-c & =3 \\\\ c & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504612 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504497 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504612 1069 "q-138809" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504612 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504612 1071 "Consider the function $y=(\\sqrt{x})^{x}.$ Its derivative is given by \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2}{(\\sqrt{x})}^{x}\\cdot g(x).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504612 1072 #uuid "22063a8c-2673-5c08-b0b7-4fcd50355c5f" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504612 1074 "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=(\\sqrt{x})^{x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\sqrt{x})^{x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{x})) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(x)^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{x}))+(x)\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{x}))}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{x})+x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{x})+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{2}y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=(\\sqrt{x})^{x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{x})^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+1).$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504613 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504484 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504613 1069 "q-152787" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504613 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504613 1071 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}/3}(12\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+\\pi ).$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504613 1072 #uuid "275cbf3f-d183-51d1-83d2-3cab6277b499" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504613 1074 "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x):$\n\n![](images/fc/fc06a4e63f38a315dd3944f65f871cca2dbdddee26ec268e857f79e5f4ab8848.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$  \n\nNow, substituting $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ directly into the limit, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}/3}(12\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+\\pi ) & =12\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})+\\pi  \\\\  & =12\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\pi  \\\\  & =3\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504614 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504535 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504614 1069 "q-340259" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504614 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504614 1071 "The function $f(x)$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{x+3}, & x\\ne -3 \\\\ 2, & x=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nhas an  infinite discontinuity at $x=${{field-1}}." 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504614 1072 #uuid "28d2bedc-f4b0-58dc-80a4-886e3d716859" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504614 1074 "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ The only possible way that $y=f(x)$ can have vertical asymptotes is if  $-\\frac{3}{x+3}$ grows without bound within its respective subdomain.\n\nWe compute the asymptotes of $-\\frac{3}{x+3}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\nx+3=0\\,⟹\\,x=-3.\n$$\n\nAlthough both branches of the function are defined for $x\\ne -3,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{-}}(-\\frac{3}{x+3}) & =\\infty , \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{+}}(-\\frac{3}{x+3}) & =-\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=-3$ is indeed an infinite discontinuity.\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $y=f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/48/48610f07da2103a0e1416318c1c70da426d8c845f0870387836098287d83efb8.png)" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504615 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504491 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504615 1069 "q-152783" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504615 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504615 1071 "What is the value of ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi }(\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)+1)^{3}?$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504615 1072 #uuid "2ac5902a-7a9e-53bd-a352-8c43204e3720" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504615 1074 "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x):$\n\n![](images/9d/9dcc5a116c2a564547283783a7df7b1cd3a9ab3b76ac24dc2695c4cb367796d5.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in [-1,1].$ Since $\\pi \\notin [-1,1],$ this means that $\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\pi )$ is undefined.\n\nTherefore, our limit is undefined, too." 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504616 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504548 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504616 1069 "q-94622" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504616 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504616 1071 "Find the median of the random variable $Y$ if the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-y},\\, & y\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504616 1072 #uuid "328730e5-b32c-5f55-aef8-1fdf6d6f55ce" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504616 1074 "The median of a random variable $Y$ is the number $m$ such that\n\n$$\nP(Y\\le m)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\le m) & ={\\int }_{0}^{m}{e}^{-y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{e}^{-y}{|}_{0}^{m} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the median is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-{e}^{-m} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {e}^{-m} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -m & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ -m & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ m & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504617 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504563 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504617 1069 "q-342333" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504617 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504617 1071 "The function $f(x)$ is defined for every real number $x$ and is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}6k-20{x}^{2}, & x\\ne 1, \\\\ -8, & x=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1,$ what is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$ \n{{field-1}}" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504617 1072 #uuid "354ae055-47d1-5393-8d47-099f76806cac" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504617 1074 "From the definition of $f(x),$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to 1}(6k-20{x}^{2})=6k-20.\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=1,$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x) & =f(1) \\\\ 6k-20 & =-8 \\\\ 6k & =12 \\\\ k & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting $k=2$ removes the point discontinuity." 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504618 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504552 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504618 1069 "q-138791" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504618 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504618 1071 "Consider the function $y={x}^{5{x}^{2}-2}.$ Its derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{5{x}^{2}-3}\\cdot g(x).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504618 1072 #uuid "358ad886-8781-5867-add0-1dfbbd99e7fd" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504618 1074 "We have the function \n\n$$\ny={x}^{5{x}^{2}-2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{5{x}^{2}-2}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =(5{x}^{2}-2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}((5{x}^{2}-2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{x}^{2}-2)\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+(5{x}^{2}-2)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =10x\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x+(5{x}^{2}-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =10x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{5{x}^{2}-2}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y(\\frac{5{x}^{2}-2}{x}+10x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y={x}^{5{x}^{2}-2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={x}^{5{x}^{2}-2}(\\frac{5{x}^{2}-2}{x}+10x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & ={x}^{5{x}^{2}-2}(\\frac{5{x}^{2}-2}{x}+\\frac{10{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}) \\\\  & ={x}^{5{x}^{2}-3}(5{x}^{2}+10{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)=5{x}^{2}+10{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2.$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504619 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504489 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504619 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504504 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504619 1069 "q-346198" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504619 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504619 1071 "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[{log}_{2}\\,(5(3+\\frac{4k}{n}))\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\n$$\n\n$\\,f(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and \n$\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504619 1072 #uuid "368aa1bb-68dd-5e11-83de-3c6725fca7d6" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504619 1074 "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=3.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{4}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{n}=\\frac{b-3}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=7.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=3+\\frac{4k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})={log}_{2}\\,(5{x}_{k}).$ By replacing the sample point ${x}_{k}$ with the variable $x,$ we find the original function.\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{7}{log}_{2}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[{log}_{2}\\,(5(3+\\frac{4k}{n}))\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \n\n$$\nf(x)={log}_{2}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{and}\\,b=7.\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504620 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504494 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504620 1069 "q-48495" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504620 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504620 1071 "The function $h(x)$ is defined for every real number $x$ and is given by\n\n$$\nh(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(x-c)^{2}+1, & x\\ne 2, \\\\ 2, & x=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$, what are the possible values of $c?$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504620 1072 #uuid "3bf93bdf-40a6-507b-8e4d-2fa269000a75" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504620 1074 "From the definition of $h(x),$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}h(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}((x-c)^{2}+1) \\\\  & =(2-c)^{2}+1 \\\\  & ={c}^{2}-4c+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $h(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}h(x) & =h(2) \\\\ {c}^{2}-4c+5 & =2 \\\\ {c}^{2}-4c+3 & =0 \\\\ (c-3)(c-1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, $c=3$ or $c=1.$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504621 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504600 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504621 1069 "q-48417" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504621 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504621 1071 "The function $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x+k}-\\sqrt{k}}, & x\\ne 0, \\\\ 18, & x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$, what is the value of $k?$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504621 1072 #uuid "3c7cc171-f78e-5279-bfdd-075dc2385743" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504621 1074 "From the definition of $f(x),$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x+k}-\\sqrt{k}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x+k}-\\sqrt{k}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{x+k}+\\sqrt{k}}{\\sqrt{x+k}+\\sqrt{k}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(\\sqrt{x+k}+\\sqrt{k})}{x+k-k} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\cancel{x}(\\sqrt{x+k}+\\sqrt{k})}{\\cancel{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\sqrt{x+k}+\\sqrt{k}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+k}+\\sqrt{k} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=0,$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x) & =f(0) \\\\ 2\\sqrt{k} & =18 \\\\ \\sqrt{k} & =9 \\\\ k & =81.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504622 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504590 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504622 1069 "q-340192" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504622 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504622 1071 "![](images/6d/6d3cbdc4a0158ebc827c2f991b446f0b9464322f8596b97176af55aa39b4a72d.png)\n\nBased on the graph of $y=f(x)$  shown above, the function $f$ has a discontinuity caused by a vertical asymptote at $x=${{field-1}}." 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504622 1072 #uuid "3f8dfca9-4caa-51a0-a26a-4299455440e3" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504622 1074 "The function has a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=3.$ Note that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty ,\n$$\n\nwhich is enough to conclude that there is a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=3.$\n\nThere are also discontinuities at $x=-2$ and $x=0$  but they are not discontinuities due to vertical asymptotes." 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504623 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504507 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504623 1069 "q-138749" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504623 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504623 1071 "Consider the function $y=6{x}^{x}.$ Its derivative is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=6{x}^{x}\\cdot g(x).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504623 1072 #uuid "3fe57972-490a-52da-b89f-179c602b71c5" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504623 1074 "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=6{x}^{x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6{x}^{x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡6+\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡6+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡6+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & ={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡6)}^{′}+(x)^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+(x)\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =0+1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x+x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=6{x}^{x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =6{x}^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504624 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504488 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504624 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504554 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504624 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504592 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504624 1069 "q-346154" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504624 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504624 1071 "![](images/18/185772b358771a524b784b06aee22a623d709b15b55d9e34000e2ef106b1e7d8.png)\n\nFor the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}+1$ shown above, what is the expression for the area $A$ as a definite integral?\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $A$ | $=$ | $\\int $ | {{field-2}} | $\\text{d}x$ |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} |  |  |" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504624 1072 #uuid "4060660e-a98e-5a45-8a1f-74d923cbf4d9" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504624 1074 "We want to express the area $A$ between the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}+1$ and the $x$-axis, between $x=1$ and $x=2.$\n\nThis can be expressed as the definite integral of $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}+1$ between the lower limit $x=1$ and the upper limit $x=2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{x}+1\\,\\text{d}x\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504625 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504585 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504625 1069 "q-106854" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504625 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504625 1071 "Given that \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ has the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504625 1072 #uuid "445c4dee-b549-5e10-93bb-06205e66703c" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504625 1074 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(\\frac{1}{3}){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504626 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504564 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504626 1069 "q-48242" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504626 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504626 1071 "Find all of the points where the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-3x-4}$ has discontinuities due to vertical asymptotes." 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504626 1072 #uuid "51bf4893-7d23-5880-bdd4-7c570a118b95" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504626 1074 "To find the vertical asymptotes, let's start by factoring the expression in the denominator. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-3x-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(x-4)(x+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we can find the vertical asymptotes by setting the denominator to zero:\n\n$$\n(x-4)(x+1)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-1,4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has discontinuities due to vertical asymptotes at $x=-1$ and $x=4.$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504627 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504501 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504627 1069 "q-129552" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504627 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504627 1071 "At which points on the surface $z=xy-3x+2y-5$ is the tangent plane horizontal?" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504627 1072 #uuid "540d859a-8179-5e53-b6ff-389c30bf0b61" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504627 1074 "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy-3x+2y-5)=y-3 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy-3x+2y-5)=x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨y-3,\\,x+2,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNote that the tangent plane is horizontal if and only if the normal vector is vertical. This means that the $x$- and $y$-components of the vector must be $0:$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}y-3=0 \\\\ x+2=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the second equation we get $x=-2,$ while the first equation gives $y=3.$ \n\nFinally, substituting $x=-2$ and $y=3$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz=(-2)(3)-3(-2)+2(3)-5=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point where the tangent plane is horizontal is $(-2,3,1).$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504628 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504520 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504628 1069 "q-48283" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504628 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504628 1071 "The function $f(x)$ is defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+k}, & x\\ne 0, \\\\ 2, & x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0,$ what is the value of $k?$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504628 1072 #uuid "550986e3-6ff5-514b-90ba-699e6397aa6b" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504628 1074 "From the definition of $f(x),$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+k})=\\sqrt{k}.\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=0,$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x) & =f(0) \\\\ \\sqrt{k} & =2 \\\\ k & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504629 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504522 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504629 1069 "q-152891" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504629 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504629 1071 "${lim}_{x\\to \\,3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}))=$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504629 1072 #uuid "55ce71ca-d33d-5c8b-9d05-07e0dca2765b" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504629 1074 "First, let's sketch the graph of  $y=\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}):$   \n\n![](images/4a/4abceba56f404b0b39fbde4b1f7da0ec36e69a496c4ef52d3bebfbcb8c7d3380.png)\n\nThe domain of   $\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}})$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$    \n\nNow, substituting $x=3$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}})) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504630 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504485 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504630 1069 "q-342512" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504630 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504630 1071 "The function $g(x)$ is defined for every real number $x$ and is given by\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{6}^{2x+a}, & x\\ne 0, \\\\ 36, & x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $g(x)$ is continuous at $x=0,$ what is the the value of $a?$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504630 1072 #uuid "571bd0c6-54b5-542b-a105-2bb5a6fcaaf4" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504630 1074 "From the definition of $g(x),$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}{6}^{2x+a}={6}^{a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=0,$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}g(x) & =g(0) \\\\ {6}^{a} & =36 \\\\ {6}^{a} & ={6}^{2} \\\\ a & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting $a=2$ removes the point discontinuity." 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504631 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504502 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504631 1069 "q-104854" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504631 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504631 1071 "Find all of the points where $f(x)$ below has an infinite discontinuity.\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-x, & x\\le 3 \\\\ \\frac{1}{x-3}, & x>3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504631 1072 #uuid "57542381-13e5-59f8-9ea4-61779c51f267" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504631 1074 "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ The only possible way that $y=f(x)$ can have vertical asymptotes is if $\\frac{1}{x-3}$ grows without bound within its respective subdomain.\n\nWe compute the vertical asymptotes of $\\frac{1}{x-3}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\nx-3=0\\,⟹\\,x=3.\n$$\n\nAlthough this branch of the function is defined for $x>3,$ we have that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}(\\frac{1}{x-3})=\\infty ,\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=3$ is indeed an infinite discontinuity.\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $y=f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/13/13817b066347391aae800b751c50a7a411dc06372d346e0d9b87e6f3fe3ca1ba.png)" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504632 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504596 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504632 1069 "q-113883" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504632 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504632 1071 "What is ${\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a left Riemann sum?" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504632 1072 #uuid "5b26923d-aa9d-5c1e-919b-9d716067cb9d" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504632 1074 "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)={x}^{4},$ the lower bound is $a=1,$ and the upper bound is $b=2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-1}{n}=\\frac{1}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(1+i\\cdot \\frac{1}{n})\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(1+\\frac{i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{(1+\\frac{i}{n})}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504633 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504599 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504633 1069 "q-129479" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504633 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504633 1071 "Find the equation of the tangent hyperplane to the hypersurface $w={x}^{2}+yz$ at the point $(-1,1,1,2).$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504633 1072 #uuid "5d1f491f-4f62-54c6-804f-2430afabf623" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504633 1074 "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the hypersurface $w=f(x,y,z)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0},{w}_{0})$ on the hypersurface can be written as\n\n$$\nw-{w}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(z-{z}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂z}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+yz)=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+yz)=z \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+yz)=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y,z)=(-1,1,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(-1,1,1) & =2(-1)=-2 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(-1,1,1) & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z}(-1,1,1) & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent hyperplane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w-2 & =(x-(-1))\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(-1,1,1)+(y-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(-1,1,1)+(z-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂z}(-1,1,1) \\\\ w-2 & =-2(x+1)+(y-1)+(z-1) \\\\ w-2 & =-2x+y+z-4 \\\\ 2x-y-z+w+2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504634 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504547 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504634 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504559 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504634 1069 "q-340208" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504634 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504634 1071 "From **left to right**, the $x$-values  where the function  $f(x)=\\frac{2x+1}{3{x}^{2}+5x}$ \nhas discontinuities caused by vertical asymptotes are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and $\\,$ $x=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504634 1072 #uuid "5f60286b-a701-5a91-9733-7afc68b9ba06" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504634 1074 "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ \n\nTo find the vertical asymptote, let's start by factoring the expression in the denominator. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2x+1}{3{x}^{2}+5x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x+1}{x(3x+5)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we can find the vertical asymptote by setting the denominator to zero:\n\n$$\nx(3x+5)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-\\frac{5}{3},0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the $x$-values  where the function  $f(x)$ has discontinuities caused by vertical asymptotes are \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{5}{3}\\,\\text{and}\\,x=0.\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504635 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504496 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504635 1069 "q-338347" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504635 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504635 1071 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=2x.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ and ${y}_{2}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).$ \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that ${u}_{1}(x)=-\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ find the function $g.$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504635 1072 #uuid "61f7a11b-44e6-5a13-bd4e-c02585b3aa3d" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504635 1074 "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)=2x.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))^{′}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))-(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+2{sin}^{2}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =2\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))}^{′}=2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}-2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)=2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{1}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{2}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =2x \\\\ -2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2(-\\frac{{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =2x \\\\ -2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-2{u}_{1}^{′}\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)} & =2x \\\\ -2{u}_{1}^{′}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)+{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)} & =2x \\\\ -2{u}_{1}^{′}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)} & =2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′} & =-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{1}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{1} & =-\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504636 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504509 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504636 1069 "q-102526" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504636 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504636 1071 "What should be substituted into the blank spaces in the following sentence to make it a true statement?\n\n\"*If $A$ and $B$ are two $\\text{_________}$ on a circle, then the $\\text{_________}$ is the part of the circle which is enclosed between $A$ and $B.$*\"" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504636 1072 #uuid "6239281f-6d61-5307-bb65-95e4ae171b6c" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504636 1074 "If $A$ and $B$ are two points on a circle, then the part of the circle which is enclosed between $A$ and $B$ is the *arc $\\overset{⌢}{AB}.$*\n\nTherefore, the correct mathematical statement is the following:\n\n\"*If $A$ and $B$ are two **points** on a circle, then the **arc $\\overset{⌢}{AB}$** is the part of the circle which is enclosed between $A$ and $B.$*\"" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504637 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504499 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504637 1069 "q-341951" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504637 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504637 1071 "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent line to the curve $y=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{{x}^{2}}$ at $x=\\frac{e}{3}.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504637 1072 #uuid "650ff22d-86ac-5784-9e2d-04a2cd40b0db" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504637 1074 "The slope of the tangent to the curve at $x=\\frac{e}{3}$ equals the function's derivative at the corresponding point.\n\nWe have the function \n\n$$\ny=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{{x}^{2}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & ={x}^{2}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =({x}^{2})^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)))+({x}^{2})\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)))}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =2x\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))+{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))+\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))+\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{{x}^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{{x}^{2}}(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))+\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of $y=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{{x}^{2}}$ at $x=\\frac{e}{3}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={y}^{′}{|}_{x=e/3} & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e))^{(e/3)^{2}}(2(\\frac{e}{3})\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e))+\\frac{(\\frac{e}{3})}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e)}) \\\\  & =(1)^{(e/3)^{2}}(2(\\frac{e}{3})\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+\\frac{(\\frac{e}{3})}{1}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (0+\\frac{e}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504638 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504569 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504638 1069 "q-107604" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504638 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504638 1071 "The function $f(x)$ is defined for every real number $x$ and is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3a-2ax, & x\\ne -2, \\\\ 14, & x=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nIf $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-2,$ what is the value of $a?$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504638 1072 #uuid "6b9e1a36-03a5-51c4-becb-f3f4b938141f" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504638 1074 "From the definition of $f(x),$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}(3a-2ax) \\\\  & =3a-2a(-2) \\\\  & =7a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-2,$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x) & =f(-2) \\\\ 7a & =14 \\\\ a & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504639 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504544 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504639 1069 "q-154336" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504639 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504639 1071 "Solve the inequality $45>-6y+15.$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504639 1072 #uuid "6bd147ae-a22b-5420-b57b-dd219e2ccfea" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504639 1074 "First, we apply the addition principle, subtracting $15$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}45 & >-6y+15 \\\\ 45-15 & >-6y+15-15 \\\\ 30 & >-6y+0 \\\\ 30 & >-6y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by $-6.$ \n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}30\\,\\, & >\\,\\,-6y \\\\ \\frac{30}{-6}\\, & <\\,\\,\\frac{-6y}{-6} \\\\ -5\\, & <\\,\\,\\frac{\\cancel{-6}y}{\\cancel{-6}} \\\\ -5\\, & <\\,\\,y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's swap the left and right-hand sides so that the variable is on the left-hand side. Remember, we also need to flip the inequality.\n\n$$\ny>-5\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y>-5.$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504640 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504487 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504640 1069 "q-154786" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504640 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504640 1071 "The interval notation  $x\\in (-\\infty ,-7)$ corresponds to the inequality" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504640 1072 #uuid "6d843358-b3f1-509e-8689-eba95475d008" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504640 1074 "The statement $x\\in (-\\infty ,-7)$ means that $x$ lies between $-\\infty $ and $-7,$ where \n\n- the left endpoint $-\\infty $ is *not* included, and\n\n- the right endpoint $-7$ is *not* included.\n\nTherefore, the inequality represented by this interval is \n\n$$\n-\\infty <x<-7.\n$$\n\nHowever, since all numbers are greater than negative infinity, it suffices to write \n\n$$\nx<-7.\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504641 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504508 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504641 1069 "q-138837" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504641 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504641 1071 "Consider the function $y=(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)^{x}.$ Its derivative is given by  \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)^{x}\\cdot g(x).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504641 1072 #uuid "73c29bd9-9c93-5235-9a81-b902322f8bb3" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504641 1074 "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)^{x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)^{x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x))+(x)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)^{x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504642 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504495 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504642 1069 "q-28812" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504642 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504642 1071 "The inequality $x<-2$ can be expressed in interval notation as" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504642 1072 #uuid "7495bbc7-a548-574a-82ac-70b0b5806589" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504642 1074 "The inequality $x<-2$ corresponds to all numbers less than $-2.$ A number line for this interval is shown below:\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/d1/d142ec418d86ccfce8f701ca19384d599ed2b5f7072d5ae84dcb3796baa73261.png)\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFrom the segment on the number line, we have the following:\n\n- There is no left endpoint - it continues forever. So, we write the interval with negative infinity as the left endpoint, not included.\n\n- The right endpoint $-2$ is *not* included in the interval.\n\nTherefore, using interval notation, we represent $x<-2$ as\n\n$$\nx\\in (-\\infty ,-2).\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504643 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504565 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504643 1069 "q-339135" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504643 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504643 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)+6}{2x+3}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504643 1072 #uuid "779dfcc0-3a36-5636-a5d6-b4ea61af4fcc" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504643 1074 "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x):$\n\n![](images/24/24f07da42a537ded7916fc2440dcf3ee1ccdfc81c7b027b47e97814fec059f5a.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in [-1,1].$  \n\nNow, substituting $x=0$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,0}\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)+6}{2x+3} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0)+6}{2(0)+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{3} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504644 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504582 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504644 1069 "q-302680" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504644 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504644 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}/2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x))=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504644 1072 #uuid "78505148-e7b2-516b-80ad-17e7629d4193" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504644 1074 "![](images/24/24f07da42a537ded7916fc2440dcf3ee1ccdfc81c7b027b47e97814fec059f5a.png)\n\n \n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in [-1,1].$  \n\nNow, substituting $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}/2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504645 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504534 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504645 1069 "q-59894" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504645 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504645 1071 "A box contains blue balls and green balls. The ratio of blue balls to the total number of balls is $1:8.$ If there are $4$ blue balls, how many green balls are there?" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504645 1072 #uuid "7b081e0c-8e04-58a2-9760-862d627853d4" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504645 1074 "We start with the ratio $1:8$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{8}\n$$\n\nIn this fraction, the numerator represents the number of blue balls, and the denominator represents the total number of balls.\n\nWe can make a numerator of $4$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $4:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{1\\times 4}{8\\times 4}=\\frac{4}{32}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there are $32$ balls in total. \n\nSo the number of green balls is:\n\n$$\n32-4=28.\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504646 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504560 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504646 1069 "q-94714" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504646 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504646 1071 "Find the $60$th percentile of the random variable $Y$ if the probability distribution function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}},\\, & y\\ge 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504646 1072 #uuid "7f1b2358-54f2-52cf-a9df-c46a72812503" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504646 1074 "The $100p$th percentile of a random variable $Y$ is the number ${y}_{p}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(Y\\le {y}_{p})=p.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we want to find the $60$th percentile, which corresponds to the decimal $p=0.6.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\le {y}_{0.6}) & ={\\int }_{1}^{{y}_{0.6}}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={-\\frac{1}{y}|}_{1}^{{y}_{0.6}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{y}_{0.6}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $60$th percentile is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{1}{{y}_{0.6}} & =0.6 \\\\ \\frac{1}{{y}_{0.6}} & =0.4 \\\\ {y}_{0.6} & =\\frac{1}{0.4} \\\\ {y}_{0.6} & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504647 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504528 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504647 1069 "q-48544" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504647 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504647 1071 "The function $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}⁡3(x-k), & x\\ne 1, \\\\ 2, & x=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1,$ what is the value of $k?$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504647 1072 #uuid "819d9389-7897-53fe-92be-f89ba9882964" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504647 1074 "From the definition of $f(x),$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}{log}_{3}⁡3(x-k) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}⁡3(1-k).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=1,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x) & =f(1) \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡3(1-k) & =2 \\\\ 3(1-k) & ={3}^{2} \\\\ 1-k & =3 \\\\ k & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting $k=-2$ removes the point discontinuity." 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504648 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504500 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504648 1069 "q-339125" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504648 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504648 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}\\frac{7\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)}{{x}^{2}+5}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504648 1072 #uuid "81d25b33-02da-5a6a-a064-091425605527" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504648 1074 "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x):$\n\n![](images/ca/ca5e8035505f20619b6707d6d1f2d0b7b2f125a004a0dbd9d448840b0e4ffd99.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$  \n\nNow, substituting $x=0$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,0}\\frac{7\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)}{{x}^{2}+5} & =\\frac{7\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)}{(0)^{2}+5} \\\\  & =\\frac{7(0)}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{5} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504649 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504519 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504649 1069 "q-154802" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504649 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504649 1071 "The inequality $z>-\\frac{1}{2}$ can be expressed in interval notation as" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504649 1072 #uuid "8301710f-dfbb-5680-866d-b67f486e14d7" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504649 1074 "The inequality $z>-\\frac{1}{2}$ corresponds to all numbers greater than $-\\frac{1}{2}.$ A number line for this inequality is shown below:\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/9d/9d43b5c54e0beb341f682f44ee4c6d42821a93d45e7d5f0c5576c13e69bff903.png)\n\n \n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFrom the segment on the number line, we have the following:\n\n- The left endpoint $-\\frac{1}{2}$ is *not* included in the interval.\n\n- There is no right endpoint - it continues forever. So, we write the interval with infinity as the right endpoint, not included.\n\nTherefore, using interval notation, we represent $z>-\\frac{1}{2}$ as\n\n$$\nz\\in (-\\frac{1}{2},\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504650 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504570 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504650 1069 "q-21132" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504650 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504650 1071 "![](images/98/98ed4353a10d4d3f799f984d3efdd4cacf50cc71a9171804779a7288211c065e.png)\n\nWhat element of the circle is depicted in the diagram above?" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504650 1072 #uuid "84e78e86-b0ee-5a67-bb4f-3d08e4417d49" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504650 1074 "We can see that $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a line segment whose endpoints lie on the circle. So, $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a chord. It is not a diameter since the line segment does not pass through the center of the circle." 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504651 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504527 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504651 1069 "q-107915" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504651 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504651 1071 "The function $f(x)$ is defined for every real number $x$ and is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x-2}, & x\\ne 4, \\\\ c, & x=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=4,$ what is the value of $c?$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504651 1072 #uuid "88e928a1-3941-5cdc-9fa6-49a8d4b468c7" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504651 1074 "From the definition of $f(x),$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 4}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x-2}={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=4,$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x) & =f(4) \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & =c.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504652 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504518 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504652 1069 "q-361540" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504652 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504652 1071 "In Sarah's batch of chocolate and vanilla cupcakes, $\\frac{3}{8}$ of the cupcakes are chocolate. If there are a total of $72$ cupcakes in the batch, how many vanilla cupcakes are there?" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504652 1072 #uuid "8ad1d634-a638-5b7b-9104-1b08e0a11215" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504652 1074 "We are given that the ratio of chocolate cupcakes to the total number of cupcakes is $\\frac{3}{8}.$\n\nIn this fraction, the numerator represents the number of chocolate cupcakes and the denominator represents the  total number of cupcakes.\n\nWe can make a denominator of $72$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $9:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{3\\times 9}{8\\times 9}=\\frac{27}{72}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there are $27$ chocolate cupcakes in the batch. Hence, the number of vanilla cupcakes is\n\n$$\n72-27=45.\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504653 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504506 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504653 1069 "q-73855" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504653 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504653 1071 "At which points on the surface $z={e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ is the tangent plane horizontal?" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504653 1072 #uuid "8c4b6aea-97fe-50e3-9ab8-7b3e7d7d950a" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504653 1074 "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & ={e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & ={e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\,2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNote that the tangent plane is horizontal if and only if the normal vector is vertical. This means that the $x$- and $y$-components of the vector must be $0:$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=0 \\\\ 2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the first equation, we get $x=0$ while the second equation gives $y=0.$ \n\nFinally, substituting $x=0$ and $y=0$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz={e}^{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point where the tangent plane is horizontal is $(0,0,1).$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504654 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504479 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504654 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504549 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504654 1069 "q-346192" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504654 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504654 1071 "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[7{(1+\\frac{2k}{n})}^{-3}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].\n$$\n\n$\\,f(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and \n$\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504654 1072 #uuid "92db8444-ee84-5a2b-a100-391911e28dfc" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504654 1074 "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=1.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{2}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{n}=\\frac{b-1}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=3.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=1+\\frac{2k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})=7{x}_{k}^{-3}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{3}7{x}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[7{(1+\\frac{2k}{n})}^{-3}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \n\n$$\nf(x)=7{x}^{-3}\\,\\text{and}\\,b=3.\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504655 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504568 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504655 1069 "q-130279" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504655 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504655 1071 "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)={e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2})$ increase most rapidly at the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{2},0)?$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504655 1072 #uuid "93366598-9424-5753-a6aa-f85435a0ef2f" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504655 1074 "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2}))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2}))\\,j \\\\  & =2x{e}^{2y}{sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})\\,i+2{e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2})\\,j \\\\ ∇f(\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{2},0) & =2(\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{2}){e}^{0}{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,i+2{e}^{0}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,j \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,i+2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $2\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,i+2\\,j.$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504656 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504581 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504656 1069 "q-341907" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504656 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504656 1071 "Given that $y=(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x))^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}$, find ${y}^{′}.$\n\n${y}^{′}=(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x))^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x))\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$)$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504656 1072 #uuid "965ab36a-7265-52be-bed3-0fcb25681407" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504656 1074 "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x))^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x))^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x))) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x)))+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x)))}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x))+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡(x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x))+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x))+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x)^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x))^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x))+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x))\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504657 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504589 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504657 1069 "q-341719" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504657 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504657 1071 "Given that $y=(5{x}^{2}+2x)^{x}$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(5{x}^{2}+2x)^{x-1}\\cdot ($\n{{field-1}}$+10{x}^{2}+2x)$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504657 1072 #uuid "9798e200-13a6-549b-86eb-d44d6cb98aeb" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504657 1074 "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=(5{x}^{2}+2x)^{x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((5{x}^{2}+2x)^{x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5{x}^{2}+2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5{x}^{2}+2x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5{x}^{2}+2x))+(x)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5{x}^{2}+2x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(5{x}^{2}+2x)+x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{5{x}^{2}+2x}\\cdot (10x+2)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5{x}^{2}+2x)+\\frac{10{x}^{2}+2x}{5{x}^{2}+2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5{x}^{2}+2x)+\\frac{10{x}^{2}+2x}{5{x}^{2}+2x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=(5{x}^{2}+2x)^{x}:$    \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(5{x}^{2}+2x)^{x}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5{x}^{2}+2x)+\\frac{10{x}^{2}+2x}{5{x}^{2}+2x}) \\\\  & =(5{x}^{2}+2x)^{x}\\cdot (\\frac{(5{x}^{2}+2x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5{x}^{2}+2x)}{5{x}^{2}+2x}+\\frac{10{x}^{2}+2x}{5{x}^{2}+2x}) \\\\  & =(5{x}^{2}+2x)^{x-1}\\cdot ((5{x}^{2}+2x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5{x}^{2}+2x)+10{x}^{2}+2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504658 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504512 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504658 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504593 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504658 1069 "q-346200" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504658 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504658 1071 "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{(1+\\frac{4k}{n})+1}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\n$$\n\n$\\,f(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and \n$\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504658 1072 #uuid "9831af65-2b7f-5270-b7a1-fe24be0274fd" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504658 1074 "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=1.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{4}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{n}=\\frac{b-1}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=5.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=1+\\frac{4k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})=\\sqrt{{x}_{k}+1}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{5}\\sqrt{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{(1+\\frac{4k}{n})+1}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\sqrt{x+1}\\,\\text{and}\\,b=5.\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504659 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504571 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504659 1069 "q-48247" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504659 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504659 1071 "Find all of the points where the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+3x+2}{{x}^{2}-2x-3}$ has infinite discontinuities." 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504659 1072 #uuid "a3713990-073a-5c59-933e-791d16a2c49e" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504659 1074 "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$\n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes, let's start by factoring the expressions in the numerator and denominator. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+3x+2}{{x}^{2}-2x-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)(x+2)}{(x+1)(x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(x+1)}(x+2)}{\\cancel{(x+1)}(x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+2}{x-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\nx-3=0\\,⟹\\,x=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has an infinite discontinuity at $x=3$ only." 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504660 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504530 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504660 1069 "q-154778" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504660 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504660 1071 "The inequality $x>\\frac{1}{2}$ can be expressed in interval notation as" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504660 1072 #uuid "a4783dad-574a-5127-8f16-79c4f5746713" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504660 1074 "The inequality $x>\\frac{1}{2}$ corresponds to all numbers greater than $\\frac{1}{2}.$ A number line for this inequality is shown below:\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/12/122ab4938bf6102036dec1a704cca8afa344d6ca3b9558743c3d573d16367636.png)\n\n \n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFrom the segment on the number line, we have the following:\n\n- The left endpoint $\\frac{1}{2}$ is *not* included in the interval.\n\n- There is no right endpoint - it continues forever. So, we write the interval with infinity as the right endpoint, not included.\n\nTherefore, using interval notation, we represent $x>\\frac{1}{2}$ as\n\n$$\nx\\in (\\frac{1}{2},\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504661 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504513 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504661 1069 "q-154276" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504661 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504661 1071 "Solve the inequality $-1\\le 5x+14.$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504661 1072 #uuid "a7f55f36-a99e-57a6-94a7-d395d5c39a69" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504661 1074 "First, we apply the addition principle, subtracting $14$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le 5x+14 \\\\ -1-14 & \\le 5x+14-14 \\\\ -15 & \\le 5x+0 \\\\ -15 & \\le 5x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSecond, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by $5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-15 & \\le 5x \\\\ -\\frac{15}{5} & \\le \\frac{5x}{5} \\\\ -3 & \\le \\frac{\\cancel{5}x}{\\cancel{5}} \\\\ -3 & \\le x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's swap the left and right-hand sides so that the variable is on the left-hand side. Remember, we also need to flip the inequality.\n\n$$\nx\\ge -3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\ge -3.$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504662 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504572 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504662 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504597 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504662 1069 "q-177679" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504662 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504662 1071 "The solution to the inequality $-5y+16\\le -4$ is $y${{field-1}}{{field-2}}." 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504662 1072 #uuid "a80bc2bf-80f4-5f0b-bce9-f8d616688e2c" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504662 1074 "First, we apply the addition principle, subtracting $16$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5y+16 & \\le -4 \\\\ -5y+16-16 & \\le -4-16 \\\\ -5y+0 & \\le -20 \\\\ -5y & \\le -20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by $-5.$ \n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5y\\,\\, & \\le \\,\\,-20 \\\\ \\frac{-5y}{-5}\\, & \\ge \\,\\,\\frac{-20}{-5} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{-5}y}{\\cancel{-5}}\\, & \\ge \\,\\,4 \\\\ y\\,\\, & \\ge \\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y\\ge 4.$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504663 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504567 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504663 1069 "q-138863" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504663 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504663 1071 "Find the slope of the tangent to the curve $y=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{5x}$ at $x=e.$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504663 1072 #uuid "a8502904-ea72-54be-8bde-7a62b1b58e76" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504663 1074 "The slope of the tangent to the curve at $x=e$ equals the function's derivative at the corresponding point.\n\nWe have the function \n\n$$\ny=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{5x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{5x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =5x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(5x)^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x))+(5x)\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x))}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =5\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+5x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\frac{5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =5(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(5(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{5x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =5(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{5x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of $y=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{5x}$ at $x=e$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}{|}_{x=e} & =5(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e)^{5\\cdot e}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e)+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡e}) \\\\  & =5(1)^{5e}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+\\frac{1}{1}) \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504664 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504498 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504664 1069 "q-73429" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504664 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504664 1071 "Find all of the points where the function $f(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+13x+15}{2{x}^{2}-7x-15}$ has discontinuities due to vertical asymptotes." 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504664 1072 #uuid "a9115fbc-4fac-58df-8796-79802b979ca7" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504664 1074 "To find the vertical asymptotes, let's start by factoring the expressions in the numerator and denominator. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}+13x+15}{2{x}^{2}-7x-15} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+5)(2x+3)}{(x-5)(2x+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+5)\\cancel{(2x+3)}}{(x-5)\\cancel{(2x+3)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+5}{x-5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\nx-5=0\\,⟹\\,x=5\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=5$ only." 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504665 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504587 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504665 1069 "q-98350" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504665 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504665 1071 "Let be $f(x,y,z)$ a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & f(0,2,1)=8, & \\, & {f}_{x}(x,y,z)=\\frac{2{e}^{x}}{\\sqrt{{e}^{x}+y+z}}, \\\\  & {f}_{y}(x,y,z)=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{e}^{x}+y+z}}, & \\, & {f}_{z}(x,y,z)=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{e}^{x}+y+z}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(0,2,1),$ estimate the value of $f(-0.01,1.98,1.05).$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504665 1072 #uuid "adb4921d-df66-5c35-baf2-cc613e0cb3d4" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504665 1074 "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(0,2,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(0,2,1)+∇f(0,2,1)\\cdot ⟨x-0,y-2,z-1⟩ \\\\  & =8+⟨{f}_{x}(0,2,1),{f}_{y}(0,2,1),{f}_{z}(0,2,1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y-2,z-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find ${f}_{x}(0,2,1)$, ${f}_{y}(0,2,1)$ and ${f}_{z}(0,2,1),$ using the given partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,2,1) & =\\frac{2{e}^{0}}{\\sqrt{{e}^{0}+2+1}}=1 \\\\ {f}_{y}(0,2,1) & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{e}^{0}+2+1}}=1 \\\\ {f}_{z}(0,2,1) & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{e}^{0}+2+1}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈8+⟨1,1,1⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y-2,z-1⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can estimate $f(-0.01,1.98,1.05)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=-0.01,y=1.98$ and $z=1.05,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-0.01,1.98,1.05) & ≈8+⟨1,1,1⟩\\cdot ⟨-0.01,1.98-2,1.05-1⟩ \\\\  & =8+⟨1,1,1⟩\\cdot ⟨-0.01,-0.02,0.05⟩ \\\\  & =8-0.01-0.02+0.05 \\\\  & =8.02\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504666 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504541 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504666 1069 "q-340229" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504666 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504666 1071 "The function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-9x+20}{{x}^{2}-16}$ has a discontinuity caused by a vertical asymptote at\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504666 1072 #uuid "adf0b53d-c3e9-5c25-81fa-45a5c95eb4da" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504666 1074 "To find the vertical asymptotes, let's start by factoring the expressions in the numerator and denominator. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-9x+20}{{x}^{2}-16} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-5)(x-4)}{(x-4)(x+4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-5)\\cancel{(x-4)}}{\\cancel{(x-4)}(x+4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-5}{x+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\nx+4=0\\,⟹\\,x=-4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=-4.$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504667 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504482 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504667 1069 "q-340282" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504667 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504667 1071 "The function $f(x)$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-1, & x\\le 1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{x-1}, & x>1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nhas an  infinite discontinuity at $x=${{field-1}}." 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504667 1072 #uuid "af7b8af8-e50c-5536-8725-7ed61d38a3dd" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504667 1074 "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ The only possible way that $y=f(x)$ can have vertical asymptotes is if either ${x}^{2}-1$ or $\\frac{2}{x-1}$ grows without bound within its respective subdomain.\n\n- We first compute the asymptotes of ${x}^{2}-1.$ Since ${x}^{2}-1$ is a polynomial, therefore it cannot grow without bound due to a vertical asymptote. So this function cannot have an infinite discontinuity.\n\n- Next, we compute the asymptotes of $\\frac{2}{x-1}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\nx-1=0\\,⟹\\,x=1.\n$$\n\nAlthough this branch of the function is defined for $x>1$, we have that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(\\frac{2}{x-1})=\\infty ,\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=1$ is indeed an infinite discontinuity.\n\nTherefore, the only point where the function $y=f(x)$ has an infinite discontinuity is  $x=1.$\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $y=f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/6c/6cba4d1e7cab2340e24d176cee3e03c6a103cf0c69fbf15209c5f4b92a4993e2.png)" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504668 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504558 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504668 1069 "q-6275" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504668 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504668 1071 "Solve the inequality $4x+2\\le 10.$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504668 1072 #uuid "b0992f95-7b13-5c5a-ba7a-568131e59eaa" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504668 1074 "First, we apply the addition principle, subtracting $2$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+2 & \\le 10 \\\\ 4x+2-2 & \\le 10-2 \\\\ 4x+0 & \\le 8 \\\\ 4x & \\le 8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSecond, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by $4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & \\le 8 \\\\ \\frac{4x}{4} & \\le \\frac{8}{4} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{4}x}{\\cancel{4}} & \\le 2 \\\\ x & \\le 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\le 2.$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504669 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504584 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504669 1069 "q-144248" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504669 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504669 1071 "Simplify the expression $-z-(2z-4).$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504669 1072 #uuid "b758fdc7-6117-52fe-b304-75d15ade9ee4" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504669 1074 "First, we remove the parentheses by distributing the negative sign. This gives\n\n$$\n-z-2z+4\n$$\n\nFinally, we collect like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-z-2z+4 & = \\\\ (-z-2z)+4 & = \\\\ -3z+4 & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504670 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504517 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504670 1069 "q-74690" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504670 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504670 1071 "What should be substituted into the blank spaces in the following sentence to make it a true statement?\n\n\"*If $A$ and $B$ are two points on a circle and $O$ is the circle's center, then $\\overset{―}{AB},$ which passes through $O,$ is $\\text{_________}$ of the circle. Additionally, if we consider the region bounded by $\\overset{―}{AB}$ and the corresponding arc $\\overset{⌢}{AB},$ then we obtain a circular $\\text{_________}.$*\"" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504670 1072 #uuid "b78166bd-7c7a-5280-b285-d24358ee444d" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504670 1074 "First, $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a line segment that connects points on the circle and passes through the circle's center. So, $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is *a diameter* of the circle. \n\nThe region enclosed between $\\overset{―}{AB}$ and the corresponding arc $\\overset{⌢}{AB}$ is a circular *segment*.\n\nTherefore, the correct mathematical statement is the following:\n\n\"*If $A$ and $B$ are two points on a circle and $O$ is the circle's center, then $\\overset{―}{AB},$ which passes through $O,$ is **a diameter** of the circle. Additionally, if we consider the region bounded by $\\overset{―}{AB}$ and the corresponding arc $\\overset{⌢}{AB},$ then we obtain a circular **segment**.*\"" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504671 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504481 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504671 1069 "q-104710" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504671 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504671 1071 "![](images/0c/0cfcb2f5e07b52b28cb40ed293996fa3699aab4759093513f7386a4d8c3024c1.png)\n\nFor the function $y=f(x)$ above, where does the function have a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote?" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504671 1072 #uuid "b8d4049d-0cbf-5d7d-aff8-ae877201f7a7" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504671 1074 "The function has a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=3.$ Note that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty ,\n$$\n\nwhich is enough to conclude that there is a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=3.$\n\nThere is also a discontinuity at $x=-2,$ but this is not a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote." 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504672 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504594 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504672 1069 "q-346173" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504672 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504672 1071 "Express ${\\int }_{1}^{5}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+2)\\,\\text{d}x$ as the limit of a left Riemann sum.\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{5}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+2)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡($\n{{field-1}}\n$)+2)\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504672 1072 #uuid "bacd6074-8287-5758-9454-33c120a66361" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504672 1074 "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{5}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+2)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+2,$ the lower bound is $a=1,$ and the upper bound is $b=5.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{5-1}{n}=\\frac{4}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{5}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+2)\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(1+i\\cdot \\frac{4}{n})\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(1+\\frac{4i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\,1+\\frac{4i}{n}\\,)+2)\\,\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504673 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504536 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504673 1069 "q-129449" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504673 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504673 1071 "Determine the equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}y$ at the point $(1,0,0).$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504673 1072 #uuid "bbe6eb83-f049-5d99-ba2d-54fe9f07632a" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504673 1074 "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-{x}^{2}y) \\\\  & =-2xy \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}-{x}^{2}y) \\\\  & =2y-{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y)=(1,0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,0) & =-2(1)(0)=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,0) & =2(0)-{1}^{2}=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-0 & =(x-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,0)+(y-0)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,0) \\\\ z & =0(x-1)-y \\\\ z & =-y \\\\ y+z & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504674 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504510 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504674 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504526 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504674 1069 "q-334837" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504674 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504674 1071 "From **left to right**, the $x$-values  where the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5-x}, & x<5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{8-x}, & x\\ge 5,\\,x\\ne 8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n has an infinite discontinuity are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,$ $x=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504674 1072 #uuid "bccf9705-1345-5b91-b48e-5b2d9522eb17" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504674 1074 "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ The only possible way that $y=f(x)$ can have vertical asymptotes is if either $\\frac{1}{5-x}$ or $\\frac{1}{8-x}$ grow without bound within its respective subdomain.\n\n- We first compute the asymptotes of $\\frac{1}{5-x}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\n5-x=0\\,⟹\\,x=5.\n$$\n\nAlthough this branch of the function is defined for $x<5,$ which excludes $x=5,$ we have that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}(\\frac{1}{5-x})=+\\infty ,\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=5$ is an infinite discontinuity.\n\n- Next, we compute the asymptotes of $\\frac{1}{8-x}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\n8-x=0\\,⟹\\,x=8.\n$$\n\nAlthough this branch of the function is defined for $5\\le x<8$ and $x>8,$ which excludes $x=8,$ we have that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {8}^{-}}(\\frac{1}{8-x})=\\infty \\,,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {8}^{+}}(\\frac{1}{8-x})=-\\infty ,\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=8$ is an infinite discontinuity.\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the function $y=f(x)$ has infinite discontinuities at \n\n$$\nx=5\\,\\text{and}\\,x=8.\n$$\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $y=f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/a7/a7773030c42d9e26a737ad1ed0208900101ed291189c7a1bc1430a808659ba33.png)" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504675 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504516 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504675 1069 "q-102522" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504675 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504675 1071 "![](images/1d/1dc519a8face19a4950e85b698e61e19f2a9f6d9e4ca8ecb3744707c43d4b7d3.png)\n\nWhat element of the circle is depicted in the diagram above?" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504675 1072 #uuid "c18974ec-97d7-5314-ad7a-0d98ada7186c" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504675 1074 "We can see that $\\overset{―}{JK}$ is a line segment whose endpoints lie on the circle. So, $\\overset{―}{JK}$ is a chord. It is not a diameter since the line segment does not pass through the center of the circle." 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504676 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504540 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504676 1069 "q-125715" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504676 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504676 1071 "Find the interquartile range of the random variable $X$ if the cumulative distribution function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1, \\\\ 1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504676 1072 #uuid "c3057bfc-a8d8-5a19-9075-be23799b04aa" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504676 1074 "To find the interquartile range, we first need to find the lower quartile and the upper quartile.\n\nThe lower quartile of a random variable $X$ is the number ${q}_{1}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le {q}_{1})=\\frac{1}{4},\n$$\n\nand the upper quartile is the number ${q}_{3}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le {q}_{3})=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the lower quartile:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {q}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ F({q}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 1-\\frac{1}{{q}_{1}^{6}} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{q}_{1}^{6}} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{q}_{1}} & =\\sqrt[6]{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\ {q}_{1} & =\\sqrt[6]{\\frac{4}{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the upper quartile:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {q}_{3}) & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ F({q}_{3}) & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 1-\\frac{1}{{q}_{3}^{6}} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{q}_{3}^{6}} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{q}_{1}} & =\\sqrt[6]{\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\ {q}_{1} & =\\sqrt[6]{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the interquartile range is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}IQR & ={q}_{3}-{q}_{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[6]{4}-\\sqrt[6]{\\frac{4}{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504677 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504505 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504677 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504555 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504677 1069 "q-342456" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504677 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504677 1071 "The function $f(x)$ is defined for every real number $x$ and is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(a-x)^{2}-a, & x\\ne -3, \\\\ 9, & x=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nIf $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-3,$ **in ascending order**, what are the possible values of $a?$\n\n$a=$ \n{{field-1}}\nor\n$a=$ \n{{field-2}}" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504677 1072 #uuid "c4cdcaf7-2982-5947-b7f9-f0933f5cb1a5" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504677 1074 "From the definition of $f(x),$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -3}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -3}((a-x)^{2}-a) \\\\  & =(a+3)^{2}-a \\\\  & ={a}^{2}+5a+9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-3,$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -3}f(x) & =f(-3) \\\\ {a}^{2}+5a+9 & =9 \\\\ {a}^{2}+5a & =0 \\\\ a(a+5) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, $a=-5$ or $a=0.$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504678 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504577 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504678 1069 "q-104708" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504678 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504678 1071 "![](images/66/661520d112c7c7ef1c4591c3708f28fdb900d9ae6d036f774593239318205e09.png)\n\nFor the function $y=f(x)$ above, where does the function have a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote?" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504678 1072 #uuid "c798fdda-953e-5d91-a2f9-32a46093ebbe" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504678 1074 "The function has discontinuities due to vertical asymptotes at $x=\\pm 2.$ Note that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty ,\n$$\n\nwhich is enough to conclude that there is a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=-2.$ Similarly\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)=\\infty ,\n$$\n\nwhich is enough to conclude that there is a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=2.$\n\nThere is also a discontinuity at $x=0,$ but this is not a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote." 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504679 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504595 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504679 1069 "q-104715" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504679 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504679 1071 "Find all of the points where the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-5x}$ has infinite discontinuities." 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504679 1072 #uuid "c899081f-aaf3-5d73-a009-6354d1a2e255" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504679 1074 "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ \n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes, let's start by factoring the expression in the denominator. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-5x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x(x-5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we can find the vertical asymptotes by setting the denominator to zero:\n\n$$\nx(x-5)=0\\,⟹\\,x=0,5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has infinite discontinuities at $x=0$ and $x=5.$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504680 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504579 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504680 1069 "q-94793" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504680 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504680 1071 "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 2\\end{aligned}].$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=9$\n\nII. ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite\n\nIII. $A$ is indefinite" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504680 1072 #uuid "c8c9dba6-d928-5ac1-ba3e-1db8d33cfb50" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504680 1074 "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the characteristic equation of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 7 \\\\ 7 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-49 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda -45 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-5,\\,9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Since $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is indefinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504681 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504573 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504681 1069 "q-154838" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504681 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504681 1071 "The interval notation $z\\in (-\\infty ,26]$ corresponds to the inequality" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504681 1072 #uuid "ca3bed50-c15f-520c-b7d3-e53cd1fcae8f" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504681 1074 "The statement $z\\in (-\\infty ,26]$ means that $z$ lies between $-\\infty $ and $26,$ where \n\n- the left endpoint $-\\infty $ is *not* included, and\n\n- the right endpoint $26$ *is* included.\n\nTherefore, the inequality represented by this interval is \n\n$$\n-\\infty <z\\le 26.\n$$\n\nHowever, since all numbers are greater than negative infinity, it suffices to write \n\n$$\nz\\le 26.\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504682 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504574 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504682 1069 "q-138840" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504682 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504682 1071 "Consider the function $y=({x}^{2}+3x)^{x}.$ Its derivative is given by \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=({x}^{2}+3x)^{x-1}\\cdot g(x).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504682 1072 #uuid "cdcbfec1-c9f8-5550-af44-736f646a25ea" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504682 1074 "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=({x}^{2}+3x)^{x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+3x)^{x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3x))+(x)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3x)+x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+3x}\\cdot 2x+3) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3x)+\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3x}{{x}^{2}+3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3x)+\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3x}{{x}^{2}+3x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=({x}^{2}+3x)^{x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =({x}^{2}+3x)^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3x)+\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3x}{{x}^{2}+3x}) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+3x)^{x}(\\frac{({x}^{2}+3x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3x)}{{x}^{2}+3x}+\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3x}{{x}^{2}+3x}) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+3x)^{x-1}(({x}^{2}+3x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3x)+(2{x}^{2}+3x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $g(x)=({x}^{2}+3x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3x)+(2{x}^{2}+3x).$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504683 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504486 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504683 1069 "q-340248" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504683 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504683 1071 "The function $f(x)=\\frac{5-x}{3{x}^{2}-10x-25}$ has a discontinuity caused by a vertical asymptote at\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504683 1072 #uuid "cdf00b6e-c9c6-53a7-abba-5979171f25e7" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504683 1074 "To find the vertical asymptotes, let's start by factoring the expressions in the numerator and denominator. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{5-x}{3{x}^{2}-10x-25} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(x-5)}{(3x+5)(x-5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\cancel{(x-5)}}{(3x+5)\\cancel{(x-5)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{3x+5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\n3x+5=0\\,⟹\\,x=-\\frac{5}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=-\\frac{5}{3}.$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504684 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504545 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504684 1069 "q-339480" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504684 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504684 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\frac{1}{2}}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x))=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504684 1072 #uuid "cdfb619c-431d-5983-938b-5503bb7b30f1" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504684 1074 "![](images/85/85a182aabfe64c27eaafde94e3bdbede9409b02cfd87395cb2fc5543b09c4e05.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in [-1,1].$\n\nNow, substituting $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\frac{1}{2}}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504685 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504566 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504685 1069 "q-113932" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504685 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504685 1071 "What is ${\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504685 1072 #uuid "d07197e2-a19c-5121-ad34-53964d711400" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504685 1074 "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)={x}^{5},$ the lower bound is $a=1$ and the upper bound is $b=2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-1}{n}=\\frac{1}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(1+k\\cdot \\frac{1}{n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(1+\\frac{k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{(1+\\frac{k}{n})}^{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504686 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504561 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504686 1069 "q-344539" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504686 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504686 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }6\\,(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3-2x))=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504686 1072 #uuid "d0a1a56f-e520-52c2-bfca-2937cd7b17ca" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504686 1074 "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3-2x):$ \n\n![](images/87/873a6fb793d2ae68eab172132e467a48a43ab84733ad92155fe6cc012f516382.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3-2x)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Moreover, from the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3-2x)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }6\\,(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3-2x))=6\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2})=3\\pi .\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504687 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504539 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504687 1069 "q-144242" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504687 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504687 1071 "Simplify the expression $-5-(2t-1).$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504687 1072 #uuid "d29eb87c-44a4-5a37-bf93-591857617749" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504687 1074 "First, we remove the parentheses by distributing the negative sign. This gives\n\n$$\n-5-2t+1\n$$\n\nFinally, we collect like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5-2t+1 & = \\\\ (-5+1)-2t & = \\\\ -4-2t & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504688 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504524 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504688 1069 "q-113931" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504688 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504688 1071 "What is ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504688 1072 #uuid "d31b1ab6-50b9-57eb-ba32-5357e77d62ee" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504688 1074 "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ the lower bound is $a=0$ and the upper bound is $b=\\pi .$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{\\pi -0}{n}=\\frac{\\pi }{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(0+k\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(\\frac{\\pi k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504689 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504480 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504689 1069 "q-152807" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504689 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504689 1071 "${lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\pi }(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x))=$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504689 1072 #uuid "d32420f7-bf1c-5010-9d26-1f698469ec0b" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504689 1074 "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x):$ \n\n![](images/0f/0fa9338fbefb3682249a585b85d287a25868a8f137547830adf6085dae85139a.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Moreover, from the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)=\\pi .\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\pi }(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x))=\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot (\\pi )=1.\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504690 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504576 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504690 1069 "q-32688" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504690 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504690 1071 "Suppose that a $3\\times 3$ matrix $C$ is reduced to row echelon form using the following elementary row operations, applied in the order in which they appear:\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1}\n$$\n\nGiven that $C$ has the LU factorization $C=LU,$ then $L=$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504690 1072 #uuid "d4f26408-d253-562e-8b0f-c05af2f72107" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504690 1074 "Applying ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2\\cdot {R}_{1}$ and then ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ is equivalent to multiplying $C$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{1}}\\cdot C=U\n$$\n\nNow, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}C=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}C & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}C & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ C & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])U \\\\ C & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot U\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504691 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504557 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504691 1069 "q-129555" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504691 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504691 1071 "At which point on the surface $z=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ is the tangent plane horizontal?" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504691 1072 #uuid "d52f8b63-948e-53a8-93f0-88d0cdc22d4e" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504691 1074 "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)=\\frac{x}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,\\,\\frac{x}{y},\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNote that the tangent plane is horizontal if and only if the normal vector is vertical. This means that the $x$- and $y$-components of the vector must be $0:$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y=0 \\\\ \\frac{x}{y}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the first equation we get $y=1,$ while the second equation gives $x=0.$ \n\nFinally, substituting $x=0$ and $y=1$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz=0\\operatorname{ln}⁡1=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point where the tangent plane is horizontal is $(0,1,0).$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504692 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504550 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504692 1069 "q-341717" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504692 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504692 1071 "Given that $y={x}^{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\cdot ($\n{{field-1}}$)$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504692 1072 #uuid "d597eea3-7247-5548-9071-6c8d8bbf677d" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504692 1074 "We have the function \n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x(\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y={x}^{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={x}^{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}{x}) \\\\  & ={x}^{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}{x}\\cdot (x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+1) \\\\  & ={x}^{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\cdot (x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504693 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504514 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504693 1069 "q-129750" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504693 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504693 1071 "Let $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}z-{y}^{2}.$ Find the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,0,2).$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504693 1072 #uuid "d66b6ff3-5c30-5d09-a1a7-0b4d573341bb" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504693 1074 "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,0,2)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(1,0,2)+∇f(1,0,2)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y,z-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(1,0,2)=2.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & =2xz\\,⇒\\,{f}_{x}(1,0,2)=4, \\\\ {f}_{y} & =-2y\\,⇒\\,{f}_{y}(1,0,2)=0, \\\\ {f}_{z} & ={x}^{2}\\,⇒\\,{f}_{z}(1,0,2)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(1,0,2)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(1,0,2) & =⟨{f}_{x}(1,0,2),{f}_{y}(1,0,2),{f}_{z}(1,0,2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,0,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(1,0,2)+∇f(1,0,2)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y,z-2⟩ \\\\  & ≈2+⟨4,0,1⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y,z-2⟩ \\\\  & ≈2+4(x-1)+0(y)+(z-2) \\\\  & ≈-4+4x+z.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504694 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504533 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504694 1069 "q-104830" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504694 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504694 1071 "Find all of the points where the function $f(x)=\\frac{x+1}{3{x}^{2}-2x}$ has infinite discontinuities." 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504694 1072 #uuid "d79ab0ee-343a-572d-8ea7-20c9b4a3405e" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504694 1074 "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ \n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes, let's start by factoring the expression in the denominator. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x+1}{3{x}^{2}-2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+1}{x(3x-2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we can find the vertical asymptotes by setting the denominator to zero:\n\n$$\nx(3x-2)=0\\,⟹\\,x=0,\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has infinite discontinuities at $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{2}{3}.$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504695 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504525 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504695 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504562 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504695 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504575 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504695 1069 "q-346165" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504695 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504695 1071 "![](images/29/29729a2b35aa370f46f15d221764b3cf8fd6344ff475016b92a974cfb31b08c2.png)\n\nFor the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+1$ shown above, what is the expression for the area $A$ as a definite integral?\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $A$ | $=$ | $\\int $ | {{field-2}} | $\\text{d}x$ |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} |  |  |" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504695 1072 #uuid "d990e769-49e8-553e-999b-5b9c5313abd0" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504695 1074 "We want to express the area $A$ between the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+1$ and the $x$-axis, between $x=0$ and $x=3.$\n\nThis can be expressed as the definite integral of $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+1$ between the lower limit $x=0$ and the upper limit $x=3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+1\\,\\text{d}x\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504696 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504493 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504696 1069 "q-106848" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504696 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504696 1071 "Suppose that a $3\\times 3$ matrix $C$ is reduced to row echelon form using the following elementary row operations, applied in the order in which they appear:\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1}\n$$\n\nGiven that $C$ has the LU factorization $C=LU,$ then $L=$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504696 1072 #uuid "dbe20736-69a2-5914-aaf4-587bd8fc1776" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504696 1074 "Applying ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2\\cdot {R}_{1}$ and then ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3\\cdot {R}_{1}$ is equivalent to multiplying $C$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{1}}\\cdot C=U\n$$\n\nNow, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}C=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}C & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}C & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ C & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])U \\\\ C & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot U\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504697 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504538 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504697 1069 "q-154829" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504697 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504697 1071 "The interval $y\\in [-16,\\infty )$ corresponds to the inequality" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504697 1072 #uuid "df8b3cd8-e582-5e68-8862-832cf8d8a7f7" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504697 1074 "The statement $y\\in [-16,\\infty )$ means that $y$ lies between $-16$ and $\\infty ,$ where \n\n- the left endpoint $-16$ *is* included, and\n\n- the right endpoint $\\infty $ is *not* included.\n\nTherefore, the inequality represented by this interval is \n\n$$\n-16\\le y<\\infty .\n$$\n\nHowever, since all numbers are smaller than infinity, it suffices to write \n\n$$\n-16\\le y\n$$\n\nor, equivalently, \n\n$$\ny\\ge -16.\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504698 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504523 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504698 1069 "q-129635" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504698 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504698 1071 "Let $f(x,y)$ be a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(-1,0)=2,\\,{f}_{x}(x,y)=4x,\\,{f}_{y}(x,y)=6y.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(-1,0),$ estimate the value of $f(-1.01,0.01).$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504698 1072 #uuid "dfe98e1c-3b93-5422-932b-6229fbfa5965" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504698 1074 "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(-1,0)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(-1,0)+∇f(-1,0)\\cdot ⟨x-(-1),y-0⟩ \\\\  & ≈f(-1,0)+⟨{f}_{x}(-1,0),{f}_{y}(-1,0)⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,y⟩ \\\\  & ≈2+⟨{f}_{x}(-1,0),{f}_{y}(-1,0)⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,y⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find ${f}_{x}(-1,0)$ and ${f}_{y}(-1,0)$ using the given partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(-1,0) & =4(-1)=-4 \\\\ {f}_{y}(-1,0) & =6(0)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈2+⟨-4,0⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,y⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can estimate $f(-1.01,0.01)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=-1.01$ and $y=0.01,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1.01,0.01) & ≈2+⟨-4,0⟩\\cdot ⟨-1.01+1,0.01⟩ \\\\  & =2+⟨-4,0⟩\\cdot ⟨-0.01,0.01⟩ \\\\  & =2-4(-0.01)+0(0.01) \\\\  & =2+0.04+0 \\\\  & =2.04\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504699 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504591 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504699 1069 "q-48496" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504699 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504699 1071 "The function $f(x)$ is defined for every real number $x$ and is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(c-x)^{2}+3c, & x\\ne -1, \\\\ -5, & x=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nIf $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1$, what are the possible values of $c?$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504699 1072 #uuid "e241dd2f-cf9d-55b1-810e-b45db1f40880" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504699 1074 "From the definition of $f(x),$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}((c-x)^{2}+3c) \\\\  & =(c-(-1))^{2}+3c \\\\  & ={c}^{2}+2c+1+3c \\\\  & ={c}^{2}+5c+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-1,$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x) & =f(-1) \\\\ {c}^{2}+5c+1 & =-5 \\\\ {c}^{2}+5c+6 & =0 \\\\ (c+2)(c+3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, $c=-2$ or $c=-3.$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504700 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504583 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504700 1069 "q-346208" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504700 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504700 1071 "What is ${\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+2)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+2)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[($\n{{field-1}}\n$)^{\\,3}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504700 1072 #uuid "e24c9c25-519c-53c3-84e2-e60c0348d7f8" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504700 1074 "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+2)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=(x+2)^{3},$ the lower bound is $a=0$ and the upper bound is $b=2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-0}{n}=\\frac{2}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+2)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(0+i\\cdot \\frac{2}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(\\frac{2i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[{(\\,\\frac{2i}{n}+2\\,)}^{\\,3}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504701 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504598 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504701 1069 "q-38828" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504701 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504701 1071 "Find all of the points where the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{2{x}^{2}-3x-2}$ has discontinuities due to vertical asymptotes." 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504701 1072 #uuid "e2b2de31-2e08-55fe-906d-b2e8d713411d" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504701 1074 "To find the vertical asymptotes, let's start by factoring the expressions in the numerator and denominator. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{2{x}^{2}-3x-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-1)(x+1)}{(2x+1)(x-2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we can find the vertical asymptotes by setting the denominator to zero:\n\n$$\n(2x+1)(x-2)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-\\frac{1}{2},2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has discontinuities due to vertical asymptotes at $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=2.$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504702 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504578 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504702 1069 "q-94668" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504702 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504702 1071 "Find, to three decimal places, the interquartile range of the random variable $X$ if the cumulative distribution function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0, \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}, & 0\\le x\\le 4, \\\\ 1, & x>4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504702 1072 #uuid "e2b4a0b2-77c3-5ed8-8555-0cfe3f01b758" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504702 1074 "To find the interquartile range, we first need to find the lower quartile and the upper quartile.\n\nThe lower quartile of a random variable $X$ is the number ${q}_{1}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le {q}_{1})=\\frac{1}{4},\n$$\n\nand the upper quartile is the number ${q}_{3}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le {q}_{3})=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the lower quartile:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {q}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ F({q}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{{q}_{1}^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {q}_{1}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {q}_{1} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the upper quartile:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {q}_{3}) & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ F({q}_{3}) & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\frac{{q}_{3}^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ {q}_{3}^{2} & =12 \\\\ {q}_{3} & =2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the interquartile range is\n\n$$\nIQR={q}_{3}-{q}_{1}=2\\sqrt{3}-2≈1.464\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504703 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504543 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504703 1069 "q-341713" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504703 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504703 1071 "Given that $y=5e{x}^{1/x}$, find ${y}^{′}.$\n\n${y}^{′}=5e{x}^{1/x-2}\\cdot ($\n{{field-1}}$)$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504703 1072 #uuid "e9eb4864-b21d-5e66-84e3-4be25804889c" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504703 1074 "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=5e{x}^{1/x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5e{x}^{1/x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5e)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{1/x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5e)+(\\frac{1}{x})\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5e)+(\\frac{1}{x})\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =0+{(\\frac{1}{x})}^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+(\\frac{1}{x})\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+(\\frac{1}{x})\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & ={x}^{-2}(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y[{x}^{-2}(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=5e{x}^{1/x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =5e{x}^{1/x}[{x}^{-2}(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)] \\\\  & =5e{x}^{1/x-2}\\cdot (1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504704 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504490 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504704 1069 "q-29069" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504704 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504704 1071 "Solve the inequality $2x+2>-10.$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504704 1072 #uuid "f0b7f966-d726-5db3-885d-bd379b62b2e1" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504704 1074 "First, we apply the addition principle, subtracting $2$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+2 & >-10 \\\\ 2x+2-2 & >-10-2 \\\\ 2x+0 & >-12 \\\\ 2x & >-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSecond, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & >-12 \\\\ \\frac{2x}{2} & >-\\frac{12}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{2}x}{\\cancel{2}} & >-6 \\\\ x & >-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x>-6.$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504705 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504586 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504705 1069 "q-104712" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504705 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504705 1071 "![](images/42/42266841cb4c8099a7ed7a0a5f5e14bc6f517c2a83bbeee7a02427d2ba44a208.png)\n\nFor the function $y=f(x)$ above, where does the function have a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote?" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504705 1072 #uuid "f32b0e13-9234-5f0f-8ec6-055e7e55ffd4" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504705 1074 "The function has a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=0.$ Note that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty ,\n$$\n\nwhich is enough to conclude that there is a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=0.$ \n\nThere are also discontinuities at $x=1$ and $x=3,$ but they are not discontinuities due to vertical asymptotes." 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504706 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504537 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504706 1069 "q-341711" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504706 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504706 1071 "Given that $y=e{x}^{2-5{x}^{2}}$, find ${y}^{′}.$\n\n${y}^{′}=e{x}^{1-5{x}^{2}}\\cdot ($\n{{field-1}}$)$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504706 1072 #uuid "f33cce2a-3b3e-5ad7-89aa-ae8325e4e759" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504706 1074 "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=e{x}^{2-5{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e{x}^{2-5{x}^{2}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2-5{x}^{2}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =1+(2-5{x}^{2})\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1+(2-5{x}^{2})\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =0+(2-5{x}^{2})^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+(2-5{x}^{2})\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(-10x)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x+(2-5{x}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =-10x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{2-5{x}^{2}}{x} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =-10x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-5x+\\frac{2}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(-10x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-5x+\\frac{2}{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=e{x}^{2-5{x}^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =e{x}^{2-5{x}^{2}}\\cdot (-10x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-5x+\\frac{2}{x}) \\\\  & =e{x}^{1-5{x}^{2}}\\cdot x(-10x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-5x+\\frac{2}{x}) \\\\  & =e{x}^{1-5{x}^{2}}\\cdot (-10{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-5{x}^{2}+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504707 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504532 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504707 1069 "q-340236" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504707 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504707 1071 "The function $f(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+7x+3}{{x}^{2}+3x}$ has a discontinuity caused by a vertical asymptote at\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504707 1072 #uuid "f55b2c6c-0d34-5876-a0ba-01591a852f5d" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504707 1074 "To find the vertical asymptotes, let's start by factoring the expressions in the numerator and denominator. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}+7x+3}{{x}^{2}+3x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)(2x+1)}{x(x+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(x+3)}(2x+1)}{x\\cancel{(x+3)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x+1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes, we set the denominator equal to zero. Therefore, the function has a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=0.$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504708 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504553 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504708 1069 "q-104860" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504708 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504708 1071 "Find all of the points where $f(x)$ below has infinite discontinuities.\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{x+8}, & x\\ne -8 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}, & x=-8\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504708 1072 #uuid "fdd8bd57-2d2f-5ace-b80e-0473ac5a0a46" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504708 1074 "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ The only possible way that $y=f(x)$ can have vertical asymptotes is if $-\\frac{1}{x+8}$ grows without bound within its respective subdomain.\n\nWe compute the asymptotes of $-\\frac{1}{x+8}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\nx+8=0\\,⟹\\,x=-8.\n$$\n\nAlthough both branches of the function are defined for $x\\ne -8,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-8)^{-}}(-\\frac{1}{x+8}) & =\\infty , \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,(-8)^{+}}(-\\frac{1}{x+8}) & =-\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=-8$ is indeed an infinite discontinuity.\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $y=f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/ed/ed2cae2a50cb15f86ead5831990366db2048c100d71dc9d1b681b890da38844f.png)" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504709 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504483 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504709 1069 "q-338353" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504709 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504709 1071 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=2x.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ and ${y}_{2}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).$ \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that ${u}_{2}(x)=\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ find the function $g.$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504709 1072 #uuid "ffda6d3a-e6e4-5195-9f9f-69837bd2b6dc" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504709 1074 "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)=2x.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))^{′}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))-(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+2{sin}^{2}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =2\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))}^{′}=2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}-2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)=2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{2}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{1}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =2x \\\\ -2(-\\frac{{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =2x \\\\ 2{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =2x \\\\ 2{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)+{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)} & =2x \\\\ 2{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)} & =2x \\\\ {u}_{2}^{′} & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{2}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).$" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504710 1058 #edb/ref 17592186504726 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504710 1062 #uuid "01cc5e7e-84c9-528b-a9ef-d7f70750a185" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504710 1063 #edb/ref 17592186504616 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504711 1058 #edb/ref 17592186504719 13194139535445 17592186